/* Longer species accounts, in the style and length of a Lone Pine field guide.
   Keyed by species id, loaded separately from species.js, and shown in the optional
   "In depth" section on each species page. Paragraphs are separated by a blank line.
   This file grows over time; not every species has an account yet. */
window.SPECIES_NOTES = {
  "long-tailed-weasel": "The largest of the province's three weasels, this is a long, low-slung hunter you are most likely to glimpse crossing a fencerow or slipping along a brushy field edge. The coat is brown above and yellowish-white below, and the tail is long, more than 40 percent of the head and body, tipped with black. Size is the main clue that separates it from the smaller ermine and least weasel. In the north, animals moult to white for winter but hold onto that black tail tip.\n\nIt works brushy edges, open woodland, and farmland through all four seasons, usually not far from water. For its size it is bold. Rabbits and ground squirrels, both larger than the weasel itself, are regular prey, and it will follow small mammals right into their burrows. Southern and central Ontario is the range. Sightings tend to be brief, since a weasel on the move rarely pauses long enough for a second look.",
  "arctic-fox": "Ontario sits at the very southern edge of this fox's range. It turns up only along the Hudson Bay coast of the far north, and mostly in fall and winter. Smaller and stockier than a red fox, it has short rounded ears, a short muzzle, and a thick bushy tail. The winter coat is pure white; in summer it is brownish-grey. Furred feet and those small ears are the features that set it apart from a red fox at a distance.\n\nOn the tundra it makes part of its living by following others. Polar bears out on the coast leave seal remains behind, and the fox trails them to scavenge what is left. The heavy coat and compact build are what let it stay out through the coldest months. Anyone this far north should treat a sighting as a genuine find, since the animal is right at the limit of where it can survive in the province.",
  "least-weasel": "No carnivore in the world weighs less. A least weasel is smaller even than an ermine, brown above and white below, with a very short tail that never carries the black tip you see on the other two Ontario weasels. In winter the whole animal goes white. It works meadows, grasslands, farm fields, and marsh edges wherever voles and mice are thick.\n\nBeing tiny is the whole strategy. Light enough to slip into a mouse or vole burrow, it hunts its prey underground in the runways where they live and nest. That habit, along with its size, keeps it hard to see, and it is seldom recorded in the province. The range runs through central and northern Ontario. If you do find one, note it. Encounters are uncommon, and a clean look at the plain tail tip is what confirms it against the black-tipped ermine.",
  "deer-mouse": "Big ears and large dark eyes give this mouse an alert look. It is brown to grey above and clean white below, with a sharply two-toned, well-furred tail. Those same features fit the closely related white-footed mouse, and telling the two apart in the hand is genuinely difficult. It lives almost everywhere in the province, in forests, fields, rocky ground, and cabins, and it comes out at night.\n\nThis is the main North American carrier of hantavirus. In Ontario the real risk of infection is low, but the animal still calls for basic care. The virus can be present in droppings and nests, so avoid stirring up dust when cleaning a shed, cabin, or other closed space where mice have been. Wet the area down and let it air out rather than sweeping dry. As an abundant seed-eater it feeds much of what hunts small mammals here.",
  "house-mouse": "Wherever people store food, this small grey-brown mouse tends to turn up. It is dusty grey-brown more or less all over, tail included, and that tail is scaly and only sparsely haired rather than sharply two-toned. Up close, the upper incisors show a slight notch, and handled animals give off a musky smell. Barns, granaries, buildings, and disturbed ground near settlement are its habitat.\n\nIt is not native. Brought from the Old World with human trade, it has spread almost everywhere people live and keep grain. Active mainly at night, it stays close to food and shelter and can build up quickly indoors. The main practical concern is contamination, since droppings foul stored food, so grain and pantry goods are best kept in sealed containers. Out in open country it is largely replaced by native mice, and you are most likely to meet it in and around structures rather than in the bush.",
  "masked-shrew": "This is the shrew you are most likely to have around without knowing it. Ontario's most widespread, it is a tiny brownish-grey animal with a long pointed snout, pinprick eyes, velvety fur, and a fairly long tail for a shrew. At three to six grams it is easy to overlook, and it lives in moist forests, grasslands, and marsh edges across nearly the whole province.\n\nIts life is ruled by its metabolism. A body that small loses heat fast, so it burns energy at a furious rate and has to eat more than its own weight in food each day, mostly insects, worms, and other small invertebrates. That means it is active in short bursts around the clock rather than settling into a single daily rhythm, and it cannot go long without feeding. You are more likely to find a dead one dropped by a cat than to watch a live one, since it spends most of its time hidden in litter and runways.",
  "meadow-vole": "Part the grass in an old field and you may find the sign before the animal: narrow clipped runways worn through the thatch, kept clear by a small rodent that spends its life in them. The meadow vole is stocky, with a blunt nose, ears nearly buried in the fur, and a short tail about a third of the body length. Grassy fields, meadows, and marsh edges with dense cover are where it lives.\n\nFew Ontario mammals are as productive. It breeds heavily, and its numbers rise and fall in strong cycles, some years abundant and others scarce. Those swings ripple outward, because voles are a staple food for owls, foxes, hawks, weasels, and much else that hunts small mammals. In that sense a good vole year is a good year for a lot of predators. It is active off and on through day and night, and it stays out all winter, moving through runways beneath the snow.",
  "northern-short-tailed-shrew": "Larger and heavier than Ontario's other shrews, this one is a robust slate-grey animal with tiny eyes, a pointed snout, and a short tail under half its body length. It lives in forests, fields, and gardens wherever there is leaf litter and loose soil to tunnel through. Bulk and colour usually separate it from the smaller brown shrews.\n\nIt is one of the few venomous mammals. Its saliva carries a toxin that helps it subdue insects and even mice, prey it could not otherwise handle at its size, and it finds its way through dark tunnels partly by faint clicks. The venom is the reason not to pick one up. A bite can be genuinely painful, so leave the animal alone, and move it, if you must, with a container rather than a bare hand. It is common enough across southern and central Ontario, though its underground habits keep it mostly out of sight.",
  "norway-rat": "A big, coarse rodent of human places, the Norway rat is grey-brown with small ears, a blunt nose, and a thick scaly tail that is shorter than its head and body combined. That tail length is a useful check, since it separates this species from the black rat. It keeps to cities, farms, sewers, waterfronts, and dumps, and it is mainly active after dark.\n\nThe rat came a long way to get here, spreading from Asia through Europe and then around the world with shipping. It digs burrows and lives in colonies, often beneath buildings or along foundations, and it will damage stored grain where it can reach it. Sanitation and sealing entry points matter more than trapping alone. It can also carry diseases and parasites, so direct contact is worth avoiding, alive or dead. In Ontario it is a fixture of cities and farms across the south and of port towns, and much less a creature of open country.",
  "southern-red-backed-vole": "The rusty chestnut stripe running down the back is the field mark, standing out against greyer sides. Compared with a meadow vole, this one has larger ears and a longer tail, and it keeps to different ground: cool, damp coniferous and mixed forest with mossy logs and deep litter. It is a woodland vole rather than a field one.\n\nIts diet sets it apart. It eats a good deal of fungi, including the underground fruiting bodies of mycorrhizal fungi, the kind that grow in partnership with tree roots. By eating them and passing the spores, it helps spread the fungi that forest trees depend on to draw water and nutrients from the soil. That makes a small rodent part of how the forest keeps itself going. It is common in central and northern forests and turns up in the south as well, most often where the woods stay moist and shaded.",
  "white-footed-mouse": "Warm brown above, white on the feet and belly, this mouse is close to a twin of the deer mouse. The tail is a touch shorter and less crisply two-toned, but the difference is slight, and confirming which is which often comes down to measurements rather than a glance. It favours deciduous and mixed forest, brushy edges, and buildings in the southern part of the province, and it moves at night.\n\nIts importance goes beyond its looks. It is a key reservoir of the bacterium that causes Lyme disease and a major host for young blacklegged ticks, which feed on it and pick up the infection. That puts the mouse near the centre of how Lyme spreads through southern Ontario woods. The practical point is about the ticks, not the mouse itself: where it is common, check yourself for ticks after time in leafy edge habitat. The animal is otherwise a familiar seed- and insect-eater of the southern and central bush.",
  "arctic-shrew": "Most shrews are some even shade of brown or grey, which makes this one easier than usual to name. In fresh fur it is three-toned: a dark brown back, paler brown along the sides, and a grey-brown belly, with a two-toned tail to match. It is a stout shrew, and the banding is clearest in winter coat. Few other Ontario shrews show anything like it.\n\nIt keeps to cool, wet ground - marshes, bogs, sedge meadows, and damp forest edges - through central and northern Ontario. Like all shrews it lives fast, hunting insects and other small invertebrates in litter and runways and feeding often to keep up with its small body. The colour worn thin in summer fur can be harder to read, so a clean look in the colder months is the surer way to tell it. It is not a shrew of the far southern farmland, so location alone already narrows the possibilities.",
  "european-hare": "Larger than any native Ontario rabbit or hare, this animal was brought from Europe and turned loose near Brantford in the early 1900s. It has since spread across parts of the agricultural south. The coat is tan-brown, the ears very long and tipped with black, and the legs built for speed. On open ground it can reach more than 60 km per hour. The quickest way to separate it from a snowshoe hare is the winter coat: the European hare stays brown year-round, while the snowshoe turns white.\n\nYou are most likely to meet one at dawn or dusk in farmland, along field edges, and near hedgerows in the south. Through the day it rests in a shallow form pressed into the grass, then feeds in the low light. Being an introduced species, it is not native wildlife and carries no special protection here.",
  "hairy-tailed-mole": "A mole of well-drained sandy and loamy soils, it works forests, fields, and the edges between them across southeastern and central Ontario. The body is stout and black, the forefeet broad and turned outward for digging. The one detail that sets it apart from other moles is the tail, which is short and noticeably furry rather than nearly bare. Its naked nose has no fleshy star, so it will not be confused with the star-nosed mole.\n\nMost of the sign it leaves is underground work: raised ridges of soil where shallow tunnels run, and low molehills pushed up from deeper digging. It stays active all year. In winter it follows the soil down below the frost line, to where the ground does not freeze. You are far more likely to see its tunnels than the animal itself.",
  "least-chipmunk": "Ontario's smallest chipmunk trades the deciduous woods of the south for the conifers of the north. Find it in coniferous and mixed forest, on rocky outcrops, and along jack pine edges through central and northern Ontario, where it largely replaces the eastern chipmunk. It is slimmer and smaller than that familiar species, and the dark back stripes run all the way to the base of the tail rather than stopping short. A running least chipmunk usually carries its tail straight up.\n\nIt is active by day and stays above ground through the warm months, then goes below for winter. Like other chipmunks it caches seeds to carry it through the cold season. Beyond Ontario it is the most widespread chipmunk on the continent.",
  "meadow-jumping-mouse": "The very long tail is the giveaway, longer than the head and body together, trailing well behind a small yellowish-brown mouse with a dark band down the back and a white belly. The hind feet are large. When startled, the animal escapes in a series of long leaps rather than a straight run.\n\nIt lives in moist grassy meadows, along field edges, and around the borders of marshes, and it is widespread across the province. This is a true hibernator. It spends roughly half the year asleep, retreating underground in autumn and not emerging until late spring, so you will only meet it in the warmer months. It feeds at night on seeds, insects, and fungi, and puts on fat before the long sleep.",
  "smoky-shrew": "Cool, damp forest is where this shrew makes its living. It wants mature woods with deep leaf litter, moss, and fallen logs holding the moisture. It is a plain animal, uniformly dull grey-brown, larger than the common masked shrew, with a two-toned tail and pale feet that help separate it from its smaller relatives. Nests go under logs and into mossy banks along the humid forest floor.\n\nIn Ontario it belongs to the central and southern forests of the Canadian Shield. It stays active year-round, hunting insects, worms, and other small prey in the litter and the runways beneath it. Like all shrews it lives fast and burns through food quickly, so it forages around the clock rather than keeping to a fixed schedule.",
  "southern-bog-lemming": "Despite the name, this is a small vole-like rodent of bogs, wet meadows, and grassy or mossy openings, not a swarming animal of the tundra. It does not migrate or erupt the way arctic lemmings do. The body is stocky, the tail very short, the head broad and blunt.\n\nThe surest field mark is in the mouth: the upper incisors are grooved, a detail that separates it from the voles it otherwise resembles. Another clue is left in its runways, where the droppings are a bright green. It stays active through all seasons, tunnelling through grass and moss and cutting green stems for food. In Ontario it turns up in scattered spots across the south, the centre, and the north, never common but widely distributed.",
  "water-shrew": "Few shrews take to the water like this one. It is a large, dark shrew, blackish above and silvery below, with a long tail and big hind feet fringed with stiff hairs. Those hairs trap air, and the trapped air lets the animal skitter briefly across the surface of a pool before it dives. Underwater it hunts aquatic insects and other small prey.\n\nLook for it along cold, clear streams, ponds, and bogs where there is cover on the banks, mainly through central and northern Ontario. It stays active in every season, including winter, when it works open water and the spaces under ice and stream banks. It is seldom seen, being small, quick, and tied to running water in country that people do not often search closely.",
  "woodland-jumping-mouse": "Brighter and more colourful than its meadow cousin, this jumping mouse wears orange sides against a dark back, and its long tail ends in a clean white tip. That white tip is the quickest way to tell the two apart. Home is cool, moist coniferous and mixed forest with dense low plants, often close to a stream.\n\nWhen alarmed it can cover two to three metres in a single leap, bounding off through the undergrowth. It feeds at night and keeps to the warm months above ground. Like the meadow jumping mouse it is a deep hibernator, spending about six months underground through the Ontario winter. You will find it in the central and northern forests. Because it is nocturnal and quick to bolt, it is more often glimpsed than watched.",
  "franklins-ground-squirrel": "On the ground this one can pass for a grey squirrel: grey-brown, grizzled across the back without bold stripes, and carrying a fairly bushy tail. It is a ground squirrel, though, living in tall grass and shrubby edges around meadows, marshes, and woodland borders. In Ontario its range is very small. It is restricted to the Rainy River area in the far northwest, and nowhere else in the province.\n\nThe animal keeps to cover and is often heard before it is seen, giving a musical, bird-like trill from the grass. It is active by day through the warm months and hibernates through winter. It is not listed as at risk, but its narrow Ontario foothold and love of dense grass make it easy to overlook. A trill from a marshy edge is sometimes the only sign you get.",
  "pygmy-shrew": "Weighing about as much as a dime, this is one of the smallest mammals on Earth. It is a brownish shrew, even tinier than the masked shrew, with a relatively short tail, and it is genuinely hard to identify in the field. A sure call usually needs a look at the teeth.\n\nIt lives in forests, bogs, and moist openings with litter to hunt through, and it stays active in every season. Like other shrews it must eat almost constantly to fuel such a small body, working the ground cover for insects and other tiny prey around the clock. In Ontario it is scattered across the centre and north. It is rarely noticed, partly because of its size and partly because it is easily mistaken for the commoner shrews it lives among.",
  "rock-vole": "A warm wash of yellow to orange on the nose and rump marks this otherwise grey-brown vole, and it is the best clue to a species that is plain in every other respect. It lives close to mossy rock, in cool talus and boulder fields in forest near running water, and rarely strays far from it. Among the mossy boulders it often feeds on bunchberry.\n\nIts runways thread through the moss between stones rather than through open grass, which further separates it from the meadow voles of tidier ground. It is localized and seldom seen, active in all seasons but easy to miss in the broken, damp places it favours. In Ontario it belongs to the centre and northeast. Anyone hoping to find one looks for the right habitat first: moss-covered rock, cold air, and the sound of water nearby.",
  "thirteen-lined-ground-squirrel": "The stripes give it away. Thirteen alternating dark and light lines run the length of the back, and the dark ones are broken into neat rows of pale spots, a pattern no other Ontario mammal carries. It is a slender ground squirrel of short-grass ground: mowed roadsides, golf courses, airports, and dry fields with well-drained soil. When alert it stands bolt upright on its hind legs like a small sentinel, watching over the grass before dropping from sight.\n\nIt is a true hibernator, underground and asleep through the cold months, so it is a warm-season animal above ground. In Ontario it is found in the Rainy River area of the far west and at a few scattered sites in the southwest. It is active by day and keeps close to its burrow, diving in at the first sign of trouble.",
  "woodland-vole": "Of the province's voles, this is the one you are least likely to see. It spends most of its life underground, digging shallow tunnels through the loose soil and deep leaf litter of deciduous forest and old fields. The body is built for that work: a very short tail, reduced eyes and ears, and dense soft fur the colour of rusty earth.\n\nIn Ontario it holds on only in the Carolinian southwest, and it is listed as Special Concern, so records are thin and scattered. You are more apt to find its tunnels than the animal itself. It stays active through all four seasons, feeding on roots, tubers, and other plant matter it can reach without surfacing. Because it so rarely comes above ground, it goes unnoticed even in places where it lives.",
  "eastern-red-bat": "Rusty-red to orange fur sets this bat apart, and at rest it hangs alone in tree foliage, wrapped in its furred tail membrane so it reads as a dead leaf. Males run brighter than the frosted-looking females. The wings are rounded, and on summer nights it often works the air around streetlights for insects.\n\nThis is a tree bat and a long-distance migrant, present in southern and central Ontario through the warm months and gone south for winter. In 2023 COSEWIC assessed it as Endangered. The main problem is wind turbines, which kill migrating tree bats in large numbers. If you find one on the ground, leave it alone. Never handle a bat with bare hands, since a bite or scratch carries a rabies risk. Report a grounded or injured animal rather than picking it up.",
  "eastern-small-footed-bat": "One of the smallest bats in Ontario, and one of the rarest. A black facial mask across golden-brown fur, very small black feet, and dark wing membranes mark it out from the province's other small Myotis. It favours rocky ground: cliffs, talus slopes, and the forest close by, and it moves into caves and abandoned mines to hibernate. Look for it in eastern and central Ontario on that kind of broken terrain.\n\nIt is listed as Endangered here. White-nose syndrome, the fungal disease that has swept through hibernating bats, has hit it hard, and it was never common to begin with. Do not handle any bat. A bite or scratch is a rabies risk, and hibernating bats should not be disturbed at all, since rousing them burns the fat they need to last the winter.",
  "moose": "The moose is the largest member of the deer family and the unmistakable giant of Ontario's north. A big bull can stand over two metres at the shoulder and carry a rack of palmate antlers more than a metre and a half across, grown fresh each summer and shed in winter. For all that size, moose are strong swimmers and spend the warm months feeding in ponds and marshes, wading belly-deep to reach aquatic plants and to escape biting flies.\n\nTheir long legs and high shoulders let them move through deep snow and dense bush that would stop a deer. Autumn is the rut, when bulls grunt, thrash the brush, and spar for cows, and it is the season they are most often seen near roads. Give them room. A moose is calm until it is not, and a cow with a calf will not tolerate a close approach.",
  "white-tailed-deer": "The deer most people in Ontario already know. Reddish-brown in summer, greyer in winter, it turns up along farmland edges, in deciduous and mixed forest, and in suburban parks. When it bolts it lifts its tail to show a flash of white, which is where the name comes from. Bucks grow branching antlers and drop them each year.\n\nThis is the province's most abundant large mammal, most numerous in the south, and it is most active at dawn and dusk. Two cautions come with that abundance. It is a major host for blacklegged ticks, the ones that spread Lyme disease, so check yourself after walking in deer country. And it is a serious collision hazard on roads at first and last light, when animals are moving and drivers see least. Slow down through deer sign at those hours.",
  "american-black-bear": "Ontario's only bear across most of its range, usually all black with a brown muzzle and a straight facial profile. It lacks the shoulder hump of a grizzly, and grizzlies do not live here, so a large dark bear in Ontario is this one. It uses forest of every kind, from the boreal north to the Carolinian south, and often works berry patches in late summer. The provincial population is large and healthy.\n\nBears den for the winter and enter torpor rather than the deep hibernation people often picture, and they can rouse if disturbed. Treat it as the powerful animal it is. Never approach one, secure all food and garbage so animals do not learn to link people with meals, and give a sow with cubs a wide berth. A bear that finds food around people becomes a problem bear, and that rarely ends well for the bear.",
  "elk": "Bigger than a deer and smaller than a moose, with a tan body, a dark neck, and a pale rump patch. Bulls grow large sweeping antlers and bugle through the autumn rut, a high carrying call worth hearing once. Elk once lived in Ontario but were gone by the late 1800s. The animals here now trace to reintroductions that began in the late 1990s, with the best-known herd near Bancroft and others toward the Lake Huron north shore and Nipissing.\n\nSo the range is local, not continuous. Autumn and winter are the seasons you are most likely to encounter them, often in a mix of forest and open meadow. During the rut, bulls turn aggressive and unpredictable. Keep well back and do not crowd one for a photo.",
  "polar-bear": "The far northern coast is the only place in Ontario you will meet this animal. A massive cream-white bear with a long neck and small ears, it lives along the Hudson Bay and James Bay shores and out on the sea ice. Ontario's Hudson Bay coast holds the southernmost polar bears in the world. When the ice melts, they are stranded on land and fast through the summer, living off stored fat while they wait for freeze-up to hunt seals again.\n\nIt is listed as Threatened, and its future is tied to the ice, which now forms later and breaks up earlier. This is an apex predator and extremely dangerous. It will hunt people. Do not approach one under any circumstance, and travel with an experienced guide in bear country. There is no safe way to get close on your own.",
  "woodland-caribou": "A creature of the deep boreal, tied to old-growth forest, peatlands, and lichen-rich woodland. Both sexes may carry antlers, and you can pick it out by the pale neck mane and the broad concave hooves that spread its weight on snow and soft bog. Ground and tree lichens make up much of its winter diet, which is one reason it needs old, undisturbed forest.\n\nOntario's boreal population has pulled steadily northward as the southern edge of its range has been logged, roaded, and settled. It is listed as Threatened. Fall and winter are when it moves and gathers most. Many people know the animal only from the Canadian quarter, without ever seeing one in the bush. It is shy, thinly spread, and quick to leave disturbed ground, so a sighting in the wild is a genuine event.",
  "coyote": "Smaller than a wolf, with a narrow pointed muzzle and a bushy tail it carries low when it runs. In the east, many coyotes carry some wolf ancestry, which makes them larger than their western counterparts. It gets by almost anywhere: farmland, forest edges, ravines, and right into cities, Toronto included, where it hunts rodents and rabbits and scavenges what people leave out.\n\nYou will more often hear the yipping chorus at night than see the animal. It is not at risk, and its adaptability keeps it common. The main rule is simple. Do not feed it, and do not let it grow comfortable around people, because a fed coyote loses its wariness and becomes a problem. Keep small pets supervised, especially at dawn, dusk, and after dark. A healthy coyote wants nothing to do with you.",
  "raccoon": "The black mask and the bushy ringed tail need no introduction, and neither do the dexterous front paws. It lives in forests, wetlands, and farmland, and it thrives in cities, where Toronto's raccoons are close to a civic mascot. Those clever hands and a quick mind let it open latches, lids, and the 'raccoon-proof' bins built to stop it. Spring through fall is when it is most active, working after dark.\n\nIt is common and not at risk. The caution is real, though. Raccoons are a primary carrier of rabies in Ontario and also spread raccoon roundworm, whose eggs pass in their droppings and can infect people. Never handle one, keep pets' vaccinations current, and secure garbage so animals do not den or feed around your home. Clean up any latrine site carefully, or have it dealt with.",
  "red-fox": "A rusty-red coat, black stockings, and a long white-tipped brush make this one easy. Colour is not fixed, though, and cross and silver morphs turn up, so a darker fox with a white tail tip is still a red fox. It works fields, forest edges, ravines, and city neighbourhoods.\n\nIts winter hunting is worth watching. A fox listens for a mouse moving under the snow, then leaps and plunges through the crust to pin it, and it tends to line up those pounces toward the northeast, apparently using the Earth's magnetic field to judge the distance. Spring and winter are good times to catch one out in the open. It is common and not at risk. Mostly it hunts at dawn and dusk, trotting a regular route through its range.",
  "striped-skunk": "Cat-sized and black, with a white stripe down the head that splits into a V along the back. It moves slowly and does not spook easily, which is a clue to how it defends itself. When cornered it can spray a sulphurous musk with accuracy over several metres, and a stamp of the front feet is the final warning before it does. Back off when you see that.\n\nIt lives in farmland, woodland edges, and urban neighbourhoods, and it is out mostly at night from spring through fall. Common and not at risk. Beyond the smell, which is potent and lingers for days on skin, fur, and clothing, the skunk is a notable rabies carrier in the province. Do not approach one, keep dogs away, and if a skunk is calm and out in daylight, give it room and leave it be.",
  "ermine": "The smallest of Ontario's weasels, and one of the smallest carnivores in North America. In summer it is brown on the back and white underneath. Come late fall the coat turns fully white, leaving only a black tip at the end of the tail, and this winter fur is the \"ermine\" that was once cut into royal robes. The long, thin body lets it follow mice and voles right into their burrows. You might see one anywhere with cover - forest, field, a brushy fence line, the edge of a wetland - moving in quick low bounds and pausing to stand upright and look around. Activity is not tied to a single time of day.\n\nNames have shifted recently. What was long called Mustela erminea across the continent was split in 2021, and North America's mainland short-tailed weasel is now Mustela richardsonii. In the field the change means nothing. A tiny white weasel with a black-tipped tail in winter is an ermine, whatever the label on the specimen drawer says.",
  "american-marten": "About the size of a house cat but built long and low, with a sharp face and a patch of pale orange across the throat. The coat is a rich brown. Anyone who knows a fisher will notice the marten is clearly smaller and finer. This is an animal of older coniferous and mixed boreal forest, and it does not do well where those forests are cut hard. It hunts in the trees as readily as on the ground, and it dens in cavities. Voles and red squirrels make up much of the diet.\n\nLook for it in central and northern Ontario through the colder months, when tracks in fresh snow often give it away before you see the animal itself. It stays active in winter rather than sleeping the season out. Marten need standing dead trees and downed wood for denning and cover, so the best places to find one are stands that have been left to grow old.",
  "american-mink": "Along a marsh edge or a river bank at dusk you may catch a low dark shape working the shoreline. Glossy and dark brown, slender, with a small white patch on the chin, the mink is at home in the water without being anything like the size of an otter. It swims and dives well and hunts a wide menu: fish, frogs, crayfish, and muskrats among them. Most of its activity comes after dark.\n\nYou can find mink almost anywhere in the province as long as there is water with cover along the banks. Summer and fall are good times to look. Tracks near the waterline and the smell of a den can mark a stretch of shore that a mink is using. It is closely tied to shorelines and rarely strays far from them, so a quiet approach along a bank in low light gives the best chance of a look.",
  "fisher": "Bigger than a marten and darker, with a long bushy tail and a low-slung walk, the fisher is an agile climber that lives in mature mixed and coniferous forest with plenty of cover. Males run noticeably larger than females. Despite the name it does not fish. What sets it apart from other predators is its habit of killing porcupines, which it does by working at the face, the one part not protected by quills.\n\nIt is widespread through forested parts of Ontario and most active around dawn and dusk. Fall and winter, with snow to hold tracks, are the easiest times to know one has passed through. Fishers cover a lot of ground and are shy of people, so a direct sighting is largely a matter of luck. Where porcupines are common the fisher usually is too, and it helps keep their numbers in check.",
  "river-otter": "Few animals give away their ease in a place the way an otter does. Large and sleek, with dense brown fur, webbed feet, and a thick tail that tapers to a point, otters are often seen in family groups and leave slides worn into muddy or snowy banks. They are social, they mark shared latrine sites along the shore, and they can stay under for several minutes while chasing fish.\n\nGood populations need clean water with healthy fish stocks, so an otter on a river or lake is a fair sign the water is in decent shape. They range widely across the province wherever those conditions hold. Winter and spring are good seasons to look, and open water below rapids or at the edge of ice is a reliable spot. Activity is not fixed to one part of the day. Tracks, slides, and the droppings left at a latrine often tell you otters are around before you see them.",
  "american-badger": "This is Ontario's rarest carnivore, and most people who live here will never see one. Built flat and low to the ground, with a grizzled grey coat and a white stripe that runs from the nose back over the head, the badger is made for digging, and its claws are the tool. It works open country: sandy remnants of tallgrass prairie and farmland in the far southwestern corner of the province.\n\nThe population is in real trouble. The jacksoni subspecies found here is listed as Endangered, with likely fewer than 200 animals left. They are nocturnal and secretive, so the sign you are most likely to find is a burrow rather than the animal. If you come across one, leave it be and report it. Loss of open habitat and road deaths are the main pressures on a population this small, and every animal counts. Summer and fall are when they are most active above ground.",
  "bobcat": "A medium-sized cat, tawny and spotted, with a short tail that looks cut off. The tail is barred with black and white underneath, and the ears carry only modest tufts. Next to a lynx the bobcat has smaller feet and a less long-legged build, which matters where the two might overlap. It uses mixed forest, rocky ridges, and swampy thickets, and hunts mostly around dawn and dusk.\n\nYou are most likely to meet one in southern and central Ontario. Fall and winter are decent times to look, though bobcats are quiet and easy to miss. The species is more a southern animal than the lynx, and it has been slowly expanding its range in the province as winters grow milder. That trend is worth watching. Deep snow favours the lynx and its huge feet, so where snow stays shallow the bobcat holds the advantage.",
  "canada-lynx": "Everything about a lynx says deep snow. The paws are broad and furred like snowshoes, the legs are long, the ears carry long black tufts, and a flared ruff frames the face. The tail ends in a solid black tip, all the way round, which separates it from the black-and-white barred tail of a bobcat. This is a boreal cat, and its life is bound up with one prey animal above all, the snowshoe hare.\n\nThat link shows in the numbers. Lynx populations rise and fall on a cycle of about ten years, closely following the boom and crash of the hares they depend on. Look for them in the northern boreal forest, and winter is the season that suits them. They are nocturnal and thinly spread, so tracks in snow are the usual evidence. Where hares are scarce, lynx are scarce too, and they may wander far in a poor year.",
  "cougar": "Few wildlife questions in Ontario are argued over as much as the status of the cougar. The animal itself is unmistakable when clearly seen: a large cat of plain tawny colour, with a very long tail ending in a dark tip. That tail is the feature that rules out lynx and bobcat. The species was long presumed extirpated from eastern Canada. The occasional confirmed report may be a wild animal wandering in from the west, or a released captive, and telling one from the other is rarely easy.\n\nIt is listed as Special concern. Records are sparse and fall mostly in northern and central Ontario, many of them in fall and winter. If you are ever close to one, treat it with respect. Do not run. Make yourself look large, and back away slowly. Attacks on people are very rare, but a powerful predator deserves that caution.",
  "grey-fox": "Look up. The grey fox is the one North American canid that regularly climbs trees, and it will rest or even den on a branch well off the ground. The coat is a grizzled grey with rusty colour along the sides and neck, and the tail carries a black tip rather than the white one of a red fox. It favours deciduous and mixed woodland with dense cover, and here that means the far south.\n\nIn Ontario the grey fox reaches the northern edge of its range and is listed as Threatened, found only in the warm Carolinian corner. It is nocturnal and uncommon, so a sighting is unusual and worth reporting. Summer and fall are the seasons you are most likely to be out where one lives. Its foothold in the province is small and depends on the survival of the southern woodlands it needs, which are themselves under pressure.",
  "grey-wolf": "Bigger and longer in the leg than a coyote, with a broader muzzle and a deep, low howl, the wolf is an animal of wild forested country where deer, moose, and beaver are found. Ontario has two kinds. The grey wolf of the north is not at risk. The smaller eastern, or Algonquin, wolf, centred on Algonquin Park in central Ontario, is a distinct animal and is separately protected as Threatened.\n\nYou are most likely to hear a wolf before you see one, and fall and winter are the best times to listen. They range across central and northern Ontario and are mostly active at night. Wild wolves very rarely threaten people. That said, they should never be fed or approached, and feeding is exactly what turns a wary animal into a problem one. A wolf that loses its fear of people is a wolf headed for trouble, usually its own.",
  "wolverine": "Built more like a small bear than a weasel, though a weasel is what it is, and the largest of them on land. The coat is dark brown with pale stripes down each side, the head is broad, and the body is stocky and powerful. That power is real. A wolverine can drive wolves off a kill and will travel enormous distances across its range. It lives in the remote boreal forest and tundra of the far north.\n\nIn Ontario it is listed as Threatened and holds on in the northern boreal and the Hudson Bay lowlands. It is intensely wary of people and needs large, undisturbed country, which is exactly what makes it scarce and hard to see. Winter is when it is most often recorded, usually as tracks crossing open ground. Few people will ever meet one in the field. Its presence is a sign of wild land with little human traffic, and that kind of land is not getting more common.",
  "american-beaver": "The largest rodent in North America, and one built for water. A beaver is rich brown, thickset, and best known for the flat, scaly tail it slaps against the surface as an alarm before diving. That tail, along with webbed hind feet and a heavy body, marks it out from the muskrat, which has a thin, ropelike tail.\n\nYou meet beavers on streams, ponds, and lakes edged with shrubs and trees, most often at dawn or dusk from spring through fall. They cut wood to build dams and lodges, and those dams do more than shelter one family. By flooding low ground they create wetlands that hold water, slow spring runoff, and support a wide range of other plants and animals across a whole watershed. The beaver is Canada's national emblem, and the work it does on the land is a large part of why. Widespread across Ontario.",
  "red-squirrel": "Loud out of all proportion to its size. The red squirrel is a small, rusty-coloured tree squirrel with a white ring around the eye, and in summer a dark stripe along each side. If a squirrel is scolding you from a spruce with a long, rattling chatter, this is usually the one.\n\nIt holds a territory year-round and defends it hard, mostly against other red squirrels. Conifer seed is the main food. A red squirrel cuts green cones and stores them in large piles called middens, often at the base of a favourite tree, and works through the cache over winter. Look for it in coniferous and mixed forest, active by day, spring through fall, and widespread wherever those forests grow. The scattered cone scales and stripped cores left on a stump or log are a reliable sign that one is nearby even when the animal stays out of sight.",
  "eastern-chipmunk": "A striped ground squirrel of the forest floor, small enough to sit in a cupped hand. Five dark stripes run down the back and a single dark line crosses the eye. When the cheek pouches are packed with seed they bulge out past the face, which is often the first thing you notice.\n\nChipmunks favour deciduous woods, brushy edges, gardens, and old stone walls, where gaps give quick cover and burrow entrances. They are active by day from spring into fall, carrying seeds and nuts down to underground larders. Through the cold months a chipmunk enters torpor rather than a deep, unbroken hibernation, waking now and then to feed from the stores it laid up. Common across southern and central Ontario. A sharp, repeated chip from ground level, or a striped shape darting along a wall, is usually this animal.",
  "eastern-cottontail": "The common wild rabbit of southern Ontario. Grey-brown overall with a rusty patch on the nape and the small white underside of the tail that gives it its name. Next to a hare it looks compact, with shorter ears and feet, and unlike the snowshoe hare it stays brown right through winter.\n\nLook for cottontails along brushy fields, hedgerows, gardens, and suburban parks, feeding at dawn and dusk and lying low in dense cover by day. Unlike hares, whose young are furred, open-eyed, and able to move almost at once, cottontail kits are born naked, blind, and helpless in a shallow nest scraped into the ground and lined with grass and fur. The nest is easy to disturb by accident while mowing, and a mother visits only briefly to nurse, so a nest that looks abandoned usually is not. Found mainly in the settled south, where mild edges and gardens suit it well.",
  "eastern-grey-squirrel": "Bigger and heavier than the red squirrel, and the squirrel most people picture in a city park. Colour is misleading: it may be plain grey with a pale belly, or an all-black melanistic form, both common in the same population. The tail is broad and bushy and often held over the back.\n\nOntario is a stronghold for the black morph, which can outnumber grey animals in some towns. Grey squirrels live in mature deciduous forest and do just as well among big trees in parks and neighbourhoods. Active by day from spring through fall, a grey squirrel buries nuts and acorns one at a time across its range and relies on memory and scent to find them again. Many are never recovered, and those forgotten seeds grow into trees, so the animal quietly helps plant the forest it lives in. Widespread, and most common in the south.",
  "groundhog": "A marmot that lives at ground level rather than in the mountains. The groundhog, or woodchuck, is a chunky brown ground squirrel with short legs, a short bushy tail, and the habit of sitting bolt upright at its burrow mouth to watch for danger. Fields, meadows, roadsides, and garden edges all suit it, wherever the digging is good.\n\nIt is one of the region's true hibernators. Through winter a groundhog's body temperature falls to near freezing and its heart rate drops to a few beats a minute, which is why it vanishes entirely from late fall until spring. That deep sleep is behind Groundhog Day and Ontario's own Wiarton Willie, though a real groundhog emerging in February is reading the season, not forecasting it. Active by day in spring and summer, and most common in the settled south. Its burrow systems can be extensive, which endears it to few gardeners and farmers.",
  "muskrat": "A wetland rodent often mistaken for a small beaver, until you see the tail. The muskrat's tail is thin, flattened from side to side, and nearly bare, held out behind as it swims rather than trailing flat like a paddle. The body is brown and about the size of a large squirrel or small cat.\n\nMarshes, ponds, and slow rivers thick with cattails are the places to look, at dawn and dusk. Rather than dam a stream, a muskrat builds a domed lodge of cattail and mud, and cuts smaller feeding shelters called push-ups over holes in the ice. It swims well in both directions, forward and back, and spends much of its life in the water. Muskrats are important prey for mink and otter, and their feeding and lodge-building open up patches of marsh that other wetland species then use. Widespread in wetlands across Ontario.",
  "porcupine": "Slow, rounded, and covered in quills, the porcupine is hard to confuse with anything else. An adult may carry up to about thirty thousand quills over its back and tail, hidden among coarser guard hairs. It is a capable climber and is often found high in a tree, gnawing bark and buds, active mainly at night through fall and winter.\n\nThe quills are modified hairs tipped with tiny backward-facing barbs. A porcupine cannot throw them, despite the old story. What happens is simpler and worse: on contact the loosely rooted quills pull free and lodge in whatever touched them, and the barbs make them work inward and hard to pull out. A curious dog that gets a face full is a common and painful result, and quills near the eyes or mouth usually mean a trip to the vet. Give a porcupine room, keep dogs leashed near one, and it will amble off on its own. Widespread in mixed and coniferous forest.",
  "northern-flying-squirrel": "A nocturnal glider of northern forests, rarely seen unless you go looking after dark. Grey-brown above with a loose fold of furred skin between wrist and ankle, it stretches that membrane to glide from a high trunk down to a lower one, steering with its flattened tail. It does not truly fly.\n\nTwo flying squirrels live in Ontario and they are easy to confuse. This is the larger of the two, and its belly fur is grey at the base; on the southern flying squirrel the belly hairs are white all the way down. The northern species ranges farther into the boreal, in coniferous and mixed forest. Lichens and underground fungi like truffles make up much of the diet, and by eating those fungi and passing the spores the squirrel helps spread them through the forest, where many trees depend on them at the root. Active summer through winter across central and northern Ontario.",
  "snowshoe-hare": "Named for the oversized hind feet that carry it over deep snow. In summer the snowshoe hare is brown; as the days shorten it moults to white, keeping only black tips on the ears, which makes it hard to pick out against snow. The large feet separate it at a glance from the smaller cottontail.\n\nIt lives in boreal and mixed forest with thick understory and young conifers for cover, feeding in the open at dawn and dusk and slipping back into the tangle by day. Its numbers rise and crash on a cycle of roughly ten years, and that cycle drives one of the best-known predator patterns in the north: Canada lynx, which depend heavily on hares, boom and bust a step behind them. Most common in central and northern Ontario. In a mild, snowless winter a white hare against brown ground stands out badly to predators, a growing problem as winters warm.",
  "star-nosed-mole": "There is no mistaking this one. Around the tip of its snout the star-nosed mole carries a ring of twenty-two fleshy pink tentacles, splayed out like a small starburst, above broad forefeet built for digging. The body is blackish and thickly furred, roughly twelve centimetres long before the tail.\n\nIt lives in the wettest ground: marshes, swamps, wet meadows, and streambanks where the soil stays saturated. The star is not for smelling. It is a touch organ, the most sensitive known in any mammal, and the mole sweeps it over the ground and through the water to find prey, identifying and swallowing a small item in a fraction of a second. This mole swims and forages underwater as readily as it tunnels, and it stays active year-round. Found in wetlands across southern, central, and northeastern Ontario. It is secretive and mostly underground, so a sighting, often of a dead one, is a small piece of luck.",
  "virginia-opossum": "Canada's only marsupial, and a fairly recent arrival at that. About the size of a house cat, it has grizzled grey fur, a narrow white face, a pointed pink nose, and a long naked tail that can grip like a fifth limb. Bare ears and that ratlike tail give it an odd look no other Ontario mammal shares.\n\nWoodlands, farmland, and the edges of towns in the south all suit it, and it forages at night for more or less anything: fruit, insects, carrion, scraps. Cornered and unable to flee, an opossum may 'play dead', an involuntary collapse into a stiff, open-mouthed state that can last minutes and often puts a predator off. It handles cold poorly, and its bare ears and tail are prone to frostbite, which long kept it out of Ontario. Milder winters have let its range creep steadily north, and it is now established across the southern part of the province and still spreading.",
  "southern-flying-squirrel": "Most people who share a woodlot with this squirrel never know it is there. It comes out only after dark, and it is small enough to sit in a cupped hand. The fur is silky grey-brown, the eyes are large and dark for gathering what little light there is, and the belly hair is white right to the base. A loose fold of skin runs from wrist to ankle. When the animal launches from a high trunk, that membrane opens into a glider and the flattened tail works as a rudder. It does not flap. It steers a long controlled descent, then climbs and does it again.\n\nIn Ontario the range is limited to the Carolinian forest of the far south, where mature oak and hickory supply the nuts and cavities it depends on. Summer and fall are when you are most likely to find sign of it. It is listed as a species of special concern, which tracks the steady loss of old deciduous woods. Leaving dead and hollow trees standing is one of the more useful things you can do for it.",
  "big-brown-bat": "This is the bat you are most likely to see working a streetlight on a cool evening. It is noticeably bigger than a little brown bat, with a broad muzzle and a slow, steady flight that is easy to follow against a pale sky. On chilly nights it is often the last one still hunting after the others have quit.\n\nIt roosts in buildings, barns, and hollow trees, and it hunts over yards, gardens, and lit streets where insects gather. Unlike most Ontario bats, it holds up reasonably well against white-nose syndrome, and it will sometimes hibernate inside a heated building rather than a cave. That tolerance is part of why it stays common while several relatives have collapsed. It is not considered at risk. One rule stands regardless of the species. Never pick up a bat, even one that seems grounded or dead. A bite or scratch carries a rabies risk, and a bat found on the ground is more likely to be sick. If one gets into the house, confine it to a single room and call for advice.",
  "hoary-bat": "Ontario's largest bat carries a coat that looks brushed with frost. The grey fur is tipped white, which gives the hoary name, and there is a yellowish wash at the throat with a tail membrane furred right across. You are unlikely to find it in a barn or attic. It roosts alone in the foliage of trees, tucked among leaves where it passes for a dead cluster by day.\n\nRather than hibernating in a cave, it migrates long distances, and that habit is now the problem. In 2024 it was added to Ontario's Species at Risk List as endangered, mostly because so many are struck and killed at wind turbines during migration. You meet it here in summer and fall, foraging over forest and open ground after dark. Its range across the province is wide, but the numbers behind that range are thin. Handle no bat. A bite or scratch is a rabies risk and there is no good reason to touch one. If you find a grounded bat, leave it where it is and report it.",
  "little-brown-bat": "Within living memory this was the common bat of Ontario, the one over every dock and pond at dusk. It is small, with glossy brown fur and a plain face that carries no flaps or folds. A useful field mark is the long hairs on the toes that reach past the claws, though the small Myotis bats look much alike and identification takes care.\n\nThen came white-nose syndrome, an introduced fungal disease that rouses hibernating bats and burns through their winter fat. Populations have fallen by more than ninety per cent, and the species is now listed as endangered. Summer is when you are most likely to see one, hunting over water and forest edge before returning to a roost in a building, tree, or cave. A bite or scratch from any bat is a rabies risk, so do not handle one under any circumstances. If bats are living in your attic, wait until the young can fly, then exclude them and seal the gaps rather than killing them.",
  "silver-haired-bat": "Dark fur frosted with silver-white tips gives this bat both its name and a good field mark, best seen as it passes early through the last light of evening. The flight is slow and erratic, and it tends to appear over woodland ponds and clearings before most bats are out. It is a tree bat, roosting under loose bark and in cavities rather than in buildings.\n\nLike the hoary bat, it migrates long distances instead of hibernating in caves, and it was added to Ontario's Species at Risk List as endangered in 2024. The main cause is death at wind turbines during migration. Look for it in summer and fall across much of the province, though wide range does not mean large numbers. Bats should never be picked up. A bite or scratch can transmit rabies, and an animal you can catch by hand is usually one that is sick or hurt. Report grounded bats rather than moving them yourself.",
  "northern-long-eared-bat": "The long ears are the tell. On a small brown Myotis they stand out, and the tragus, the pointed flap inside the ear, is likewise long and narrow. This bat keeps to the interior of the forest rather than open edges, and it hunts in an unusual way, gleaning insects straight off leaves and bark instead of taking them in open air. It roosts under bark and in cavities through the summer and hibernates in caves and mines.\n\nThat reliance on cave hibernation left it badly exposed. White-nose syndrome has hit this species among the hardest of any Ontario bat, with population losses that can only be called catastrophic. It is listed as endangered, and it persists in small numbers where forests still hold the standing dead trees it needs. Do not handle it, or any bat. A bite or scratch is a rabies risk. If you manage forest land, leaving mature interior woods and dead standing trees intact is the most useful thing you can do for it.",
  "tri-colored-bat": "Ontario's smallest bat is a scrap of an animal with a weak, fluttering flight that has been likened to a large moth. Up close the back hairs show three bands, dark at the base, yellow-brown in the middle, and dark again at the tip, and the forearms are a distinctive pinkish red. In summer it works forest edges and waterways. In winter it hibernates in caves and abandoned mines.\n\nThose hibernation sites are where the trouble reached it. White-nose syndrome collapsed its colonies, and it is now one of the province's rarest bats, listed as endangered. Its Ontario range covers the south and central parts of the province, but encounters are few even where it still occurs. The safety point is the same as for every bat. Never touch one. A bite or scratch can carry rabies, and a bat on the ground is more likely to be sick than simply resting. Keep pets away from it and call a local authority for advice.",
  "midland-painted-turtle": "Painted turtles are the ones you see lined up on a log on a warm afternoon, sometimes several to a row. The shell is smooth and dark, 10 to 18 cm long, with red bars along the outer scutes and yellow stripes running back from the head. They live in shallow, slow water with soft bottoms and plenty of logs to climb onto: marshes and quiet bays. You will find them across southern and central Ontario from spring through fall, active by day. They warm up by basking, then drop into the water at any sign of trouble.\n\nOne detail sets them apart. Hatchlings can survive freezing in the nest, tolerating ice in their own tissues through their first winter before digging out in spring. This is Ontario's most familiar turtle and it is not at risk provincially. It is still a specially protected reptile, so leave it where you find it and do not take one home.",
  "snapping-turtle": "A snapping turtle is built like something older than it is. The head is massive, the tail long and saw-toothed, and the plastron underneath is small and cross-shaped, leaving the legs exposed. Shells run 20 to 36 cm and large animals can reach 16 kg. They live in slow lakes, ponds, rivers, and marshes with soft mud and thick plants, spending most of their time on the bottom. In the water they are calm and will move away from you. The danger is on land. Females cross roads to nest in June, and a cornered snapper strikes fast with real force.\n\nThese turtles can live past 100 years and do not breed until 15 to 20, so every adult killed on a road is a loss the population feels for a long time. Snapping Turtle is listed as special concern in Ontario. If you help one across, never lift it by the tail, which can injure its spine. Grasp the rear of the shell, or slide it onto a car mat and drag it the way it was already headed.",
  "blandings-turtle": "The yellow throat is the giveaway. Blanding's Turtle has a bright yellow chin and throat and a high-domed dark shell flecked with yellow, and the set of its jaw gives it a permanent faint smile. Shells run 15 to 27 cm. It lives in shallow weedy wetlands, marshes, and ponds, but it does not stay put. It travels widely overland between wetlands, which is when many are lost on roads. Look for it in southern and central Ontario within the Great Lakes basin, active by day through the warmer months.\n\nThis is a slow-lived animal. Individuals often pass 75 years, and females may not nest for the first time until their late teens, so populations recover slowly from losses. Blanding's Turtle is listed as threatened in Ontario. If you find one crossing a road, move it across in the direction it was going and report the sighting to a provincial reptile atlas. Do not relocate it elsewhere. These turtles know their home wetlands and will try to return.",
  "common-musk-turtle": "Small and secretive, the stinkpot spends its life walking the bottom of shallow, slow water rather than swimming in the open. The shell is high-domed and only 5 to 14 cm long. Two pale stripes run along the face and short barbels hang from the chin. It rarely climbs out to bask the way painted turtles do, so it is easy to overlook. It lives in muddy-bottomed lakes and quiet rivers across southern and central Ontario in the Great Lakes basin, most active at dawn and dusk through the warmer months.\n\nThe name is earned. Handled, it releases a foul musky odour from glands near the shell, enough to make most predators, and most people, put it down. This is a special concern species in Ontario. If you meet one, a quick look is fine, but set it back gently where you found it and expect the smell.",
  "northern-map-turtle": "The shell is where the name comes from. Fine yellow lines wander across the olive carapace like contour lines on a map, and there is a low keel down the middle and a yellow spot behind each eye. Females are much larger than males, reaching 18 to 27 cm against the males' 9 to 16. Map turtles keep to large rivers and lakes with slow current, soft bottoms, and good basking sites. In Ontario that means the Great Lakes and big waterways like the St. Lawrence, Ottawa, and Rideau.\n\nThey are among the wariest turtles you will try to watch. A whole basking group pours off a log into the water at the first hint of movement, so binoculars work better than a close approach. Large females have powerful jaws for crushing snails and clams, which make up much of their diet. Northern Map Turtle is listed as special concern in Ontario. Watch from a distance and let basking animals settle.",
  "red-eared-slider": "This is the turtle from the pet store, now living wild in parts of Ontario. A red stripe behind each eye is the clearest mark, and adults are often larger and paler than the native painted turtles they sit beside. Shells reach 13 to 28 cm. Sliders turn up in warm weedy ponds, urban stormwater ponds, and quiet lake edges, mostly in and around southern Ontario cities, wherever released pets have survived and sometimes bred.\n\nThey are native to the central United States, not here. Every Ontario animal traces back to the pet trade. Where they establish, they can compete with native turtles for the limited basking sites those species depend on. Never release a pet turtle into the wild. It is illegal, and it rarely ends well for the animal. A pet turtle you can no longer keep should go to a rescue or a vet, not a pond.",
  "spiny-softshell": "Nothing else in Ontario looks like a spiny softshell. The shell is flat, round, and leathery, more like a pancake than the hard scutes of other turtles, and the snout draws out into a long snorkel that lets the animal breathe while its body stays hidden in sand. Small spines line the front edge of the carapace. Females are much larger than males and can reach 43 cm. They need large rivers and lakes with soft sand or mud and few obstructions, and in Ontario that comes down to a short list: Lake Erie, Lake St. Clair, and the Thames and Sydenham rivers.\n\nSoftshells can take up oxygen through the skin and the lining of the throat, which lets them stay under for a very long time. This is an endangered species in Ontario, so a sighting is worth reporting. The long neck reaches farther than you expect and the jaws are sharp. If you must move one off a road, keep clear of the head and handle it as little as possible.",
  "spotted-turtle": "Black shell, yellow polka-dots. Spotted Turtle is small, 8 to 13 cm, its smooth dark shell sprinkled with bright yellow spots that carry onto the head and legs. It lives in shallow, clean wetlands: fens, bogs, marshy meadows, and wet woodlands. This is an early riser. It is one of the first turtles active in spring, sometimes moving over ice, then it goes quiet and estivates through the heat of summer, so spring is realistically your only window to see one. In Ontario it is scattered across the south and east and around Georgian Bay.\n\nSpotted Turtle is endangered here, and illegal collection for the pet trade is one of the main reasons. That fact shapes how you should treat a sighting. Enjoy it, do not handle or move the animal, and do not share the exact location publicly. Reporting to a provincial reptile atlas is the right channel. Taking one is illegal and does real harm to a species already thin on the ground.",
  "wood-turtle": "Look at the shell and it seems carved. Each scute rises in a low pyramid of concentric ridges, giving a worked-wood texture, and there is orange on the neck and legs. Shells run 16 to 25 cm. This is the most terrestrial of Ontario's turtles. It keeps to clean, meandering rivers and streams with sandy bottoms but ranges well into the neighbouring forests and meadows to forage in summer, so you may meet one a fair distance from water. It is active by day through the warmer months.\n\nWood turtles are unusually clever for reptiles and have been shown to work out simple foraging problems. In Ontario they occur along the Great Lakes-St. Lawrence system and in scattered central and eastern rivers. Wood Turtle is endangered here. Clean flowing water and undisturbed nesting banks are what it needs, and adults lost to roads or collection are hard to replace. If you find one, leave it be and report the sighting rather than moving it.",
  "dekays-brownsnake": "One of the few snakes that does well in a city. DeKay's Brownsnake is small, 23 to 33 cm, plain brown with two rows of small dark spots down the back and a pale belly. It lives in the overlooked places, vacant lots, gardens, parks, and woodland edges, often tucked under a board, a rock, or a piece of debris, and several may share one cover object. Its food is earthworms and slugs, which is part of why it manages so well among people. In Ontario it is a southern snake, most common in the urban Carolinian zone, and active from spring through fall.\n\nThis snake is completely harmless. It has no venom and rarely attempts to bite. If you lift a board and find one, have your look and set the cover gently back the way it was so the animal keeps its shelter. It is not at risk in the province.",
  "eastern-gartersnake": "If you have seen a snake in Ontario, it was probably this one. The eastern gartersnake wears three yellow stripes down a dark body, though the background colour varies a great deal from one animal to the next. Most run 45 to 66 cm, with the odd individual reaching much longer. It turns up almost anywhere there is cover and prey: meadows, gardens, forest edges, wetlands, roadsides. This is the most common and widespread snake in the province, out by day from spring through fall.\n\nFemales give birth to live young in late summer rather than laying eggs, and in autumn the snakes gather at communal dens, sometimes in numbers, to spend the winter below the frost. It is harmless. Picked up, it may thrash and smear a musky discharge on your hands, which washes off. There is no venom and no real danger. A gartersnake in the garden is worth leaving alone. It eats slugs, worms, and other small prey.",
  "northern-watersnake": "Seen swimming at a lake edge, this snake gets mistaken for a venomous water moccasin more than any other in Ontario. There is no water moccasin here. The northern watersnake is a harmless native, stout-bodied and 60 to 135 cm, patterned with dark bands and blotches that are clearest toward the head. It lives along the edges of lakes, rivers, ponds, and marshes and basks on rocks, logs, and docks across southern and central Ontario. Given room, it will move off.\n\nCornered, it defends itself in earnest. It flattens its body to look bigger, releases musk, and bites. The saliva carries a mild anticoagulant, so a bite may bleed more than the wound warrants, but there is no venom involved. Do not grab it, and do not try to move it off a dock by hand. Left alone, it will leave on its own. This snake is not at risk in the province.",
  "eastern-milksnake": "A tan-grey snake marked with reddish, black-edged blotches, with a Y or V shape on the neck where it meets the head. You meet it around farmland, fields, rocky outcrops, forest edges, and old buildings, anywhere mice and other small mammals gather. It hunts them by constriction, which makes it worth having near a barn. Active mainly at dawn and dusk through spring, summer, and fall.\n\nThe name traces to an old belief that it drinks milk from cows. It does not. What it does do when alarmed is vibrate its tail in dry leaf litter, and that buzz, together with the blotched pattern, gets it mistaken for a rattlesnake. The comparison does not hold. This is a harmless species, common across southern and central Ontario, and one that is killed far too often out of plain fear.",
  "eastern-ribbonsnake": "Slender even by garter standards, with three bright yellow stripes and a tail that runs to nearly a third of its length. A clean white spot sits just ahead of each eye, and the whole animal looks like a stretched, narrowed gartersnake. Look for it at the edges of marshes, bogs, ponds, and slow streams, in dense shoreline plants, rarely far from water.\n\nIt hunts frogs and small fish and can move across the surface of the water without sinking. Active by day through the warm months. It is listed as a species of special concern in Ontario, and its fortunes are tied to the health of the wetlands it depends on, so shoreline development and drainage work against it. If you find one, watch it and leave it be. It ranges through southern and central Ontario and the Great Lakes basin.",
  "red-bellied-snake": "One of the smallest snakes in Ontario, running from about the length of a pencil to a hand-span. The back is plain brown to grey, but turn one over and the belly is brick red or orange, usually with three pale spots just behind the head. It lives in forests, bogs, and clearings with good ground cover, and spends most of its time out of sight under logs and bark.\n\nSlugs and earthworms are the main food. When picked up it sometimes curls its upper lip to show tiny teeth, a bluff that does no harm at all. It is completely harmless to people. Widespread from southern and central Ontario up to the boreal edge, and active spring through fall, by day or night depending on conditions. Easy to overlook, and easy to set gently aside if you turn one up under a board.",
  "ring-necked-snake": "Secretive and rarely out in the open, this small snake keeps to moist forests and rocky hillsides with flat rocks and logs to hide under. The back is slate grey, broken by a clean yellow-orange ring across the neck, and the belly matches that colour. When alarmed it may coil and lift its tail to flash the bright underside as a warning.\n\nSalamanders, worms, and other small snakes make up the diet. It is most active at night through spring, summer, and fall, which is part of why it goes unnoticed even where it is common. Found across southern and central Ontario along the edge of the Canadian Shield. Harmless. Most people who live near them never see one, and the usual way to turn one up is to lift a rock and then set it back carefully where it was.",
  "smooth-greensnake": "Uniformly bright green above with a white to pale-yellow belly, this slender snake all but vanishes in grass. Moist meadows, grasslands, marsh edges, and open fields with thick low cover are where it lives. It is one of the few snakes that eats insects rather than vertebrates, taking mostly spiders and caterpillars, which ties it closely to healthy, unsprayed grassland.\n\nActive by day through the warm months. It is harmless and rarely bites. One odd note for anyone who finds a dead one: the green pigment breaks down after death and the body fades to blue. Because it depends on insect-rich meadows, pesticide use and heavy mowing hurt it. It ranges across southern and central Ontario.",
  "blue-racer": "Large and fast, with a slender build and smooth grey-blue to blue-green sides, a pale belly, big eyes, and a dark mask through the eye. It is among the longest and quickest snakes in the province. In Canada it now survives in a single place: Pelee Island in Lake Erie. The last reliable mainland record was in 1983.\n\nThere it lives in tallgrass prairie, savanna, alvar, and old fields with scattered rocky cover. It is non-venomous. Its instinct is to flee at speed, and it will bite only if cornered and handled. Endangered, with a tiny range and few animals left. It is protected, and its habitat matters. If you are on Pelee and see one pour off through the grass, that is a piece of the entire Canadian population moving away from you. Give it distance. Active by day, spring through fall.",
  "butlers-gartersnake": "A small gartersnake with three yellow-orange stripes and a head that looks slightly undersized for the body. The side stripes sit higher up the flank than on the common eastern gartersnake, which is one way to tell them apart, though the two are genuinely hard to separate in the field. It is harmless.\n\nAn earthworm specialist, it keeps to tallgrass prairie, wet meadows, and old fields near water, including scrappy urban and industrial edges. In Canada it is restricted to a few pockets of southwestern Ontario, with the main populations near Windsor and Sarnia, at Parkhill, and at Luther Marsh. That narrow range is why it is listed as endangered. Active by day through spring, summer, and fall. The remnant prairie patches it depends on are themselves rare, so protecting the snake means protecting those fields.",
  "eastern-foxsnake": "One of the largest snakes in Ontario, yellowish-brown with dark blotches down the back and a reddish, coppery head. When disturbed it vibrates its tail in dry leaves, a rattlesnake impression that gets it killed by people who take the bluff at face value. It is entirely harmless and a strong swimmer, at home along Great Lakes shoreline marshes, prairies, rocky shores, and nearby farmland, where it earns its keep on rodents.\n\nTwo isolated populations remain in the province: the Carolinian population along the Lake Erie and St. Clair shores, which is endangered, and the eastern Georgian Bay population, which is threatened. Road mortality and habitat loss are the main pressures. Active by day, spring through fall. If you see a large blotched snake buzzing its tail near the water, the correct response is to leave it alone. It cannot hurt you.",
  "eastern-hog-nosed-snake": "Stout-bodied, with an upturned pig-like snout and colour that varies so much from one animal to the next that pattern alone is unreliable. The snout is the giveaway. It lives in sandy or well-drained soils in open woodland, dunes, and beaches where toads, its main prey, are common.\n\nThe display is what people remember. When threatened it flattens its neck like a cobra, hisses, and strikes with a closed mouth. If that fails to move you, it rolls belly-up and plays dead, sometimes with the tongue lolling out. It is harmless to people and not dangerously venomous. Listed as threatened, it turns up in Great Lakes sand areas, around Georgian Bay, in Norfolk, and in eastern Ontario, with roads and habitat loss the chief threats. Active by day through the warm months. The whole performance is a bluff. Watch it, then move off and let it recover.",
  "gray-ratsnake": "The longest snake in Ontario. Adults are glossy black with a pale chin; juveniles are strongly blotched and look like a different animal entirely. It is an excellent climber, and much of its hunting happens off the ground, going up trees to reach bird nests and into burrows for rodents, which it kills by constriction. Deciduous and mixed forests, forest edges, rock ridges, and old barns all suit it.\n\nHarmless to people. Two populations survive in the province: the Frontenac Axis population in eastern Ontario, listed as threatened, and the Carolinian population in the southwest, listed as endangered. Both are hurt by habitat loss and road deaths, and both are slow to recover, because the snakes are long-lived and reproduce slowly. Active by day, spring through fall. A big black snake in a barn is doing you a favour on the rodent front.",
  "lake-erie-watersnake": "A pale relative of the northern watersnake, often nearly uniform grey with its banding faded or gone. It basks on the rocky and cobble shorelines, limestone ledges, and shallows of the Lake Erie islands, and it lives nowhere else. Non-venomous, though like any watersnake it will bite and release musk if grabbed, so there is no reason to grab one.\n\nThis is a genuine recovery story. Once endangered, it rebounded and was downlisted to special concern in Ontario in 2017. Part of that turnaround came from an unexpected food source, the invasive round goby, which now makes up much of its diet. Active by day through the warm months, on Pelee, Middle, East Sister, and neighbouring islands. It is still tied to that small island range, so it remains worth watching, but the trend has been in the right direction.",
  "massasauga": "Ontario's only venomous snake, and a shy one. The body is thick, marked with dark saddle blotches, and the head is triangular with vertical pupils. At the tail is a small segmented rattle that buzzes at a higher pitch than the rattle of a large western rattlesnake. It lives in wetlands, rock barrens, prairies, and shoreline forests, most reliably around eastern Georgian Bay and the Bruce Peninsula. It is nearly gone from the southwest.\n\nIts nature is to rattle and retreat, not to attack. Bites are very rare and almost always happen when someone tries to handle or kill one. Do not do either. Give it room and walk around it. If a bite does happen, stay calm, keep the limb still, and get to medical care immediately; antivenom is available and deaths are extremely rare with prompt treatment. Listed as threatened, it is protected by law. Active spring through fall, by day or night depending on the weather.",
  "queensnake": "The queensnake lives along clean, rocky streams and rivers where riffles run over stone and crayfish are common. To find this snake, find good water first. Look for a slender olive-brown snake, often draped on a branch above the current, with a yellow belly crossed by four dark stripes and a pale stripe low along each side. It rarely strays far from flowing water.\n\nIt depends on crayfish, and on a narrow window in particular. The queensnake feeds on soft-shelled crayfish in the hours just after they moult, before the new shell hardens. That specialization ties it to water quality. Where crayfish decline, so does the snake. In Ontario it holds on in only a few southwestern watersheds: the Grand, Thames, Sydenham, and Ausable. It is listed as Endangered. A warm day from spring through fall is your best chance to see one, basking or working the shallows. It is non-venomous and harmless to people, and should be left alone.",
  "common-five-lined-skink": "Ontario has just one native lizard, and this is it. The common five-lined skink is a smooth, shiny reptile with five pale stripes running the length of a darker body. Young ones carry a bright blue tail, and in spring the breeding males show orange along the jaws. It keeps to rock outcrops, sandy open woodland, and ground littered with logs and flat rocks, where it can warm in the sun and vanish under cover in an instant.\n\nHandle one carelessly and you may be left holding only a tail. The skink can drop its still-wriggling tail to distract a predator, then regrow it slowly. Look for it by day from spring into fall. Two separate populations occur in the province: a Carolinian group in the far southwest, listed as Endangered, and a Southern Shield group around Georgian Bay, listed as Special concern. Both are protected under provincial law. If you lift a rock to check for one, set it back exactly as it lay. That cover is part of the habitat, and the animal depends on it.",
  "american-bullfrog": "This is the biggest frog in Ontario. It grows to around 20 cm and sits heavy in the water, usually with just its eyes and the top of its head showing. The clearest mark is what it lacks: the dorsolateral ridges, the folds of skin running down the back, that most of our frogs have. Instead a short ridge curls around the very large eardrum behind each eye. The call carries a long way, a deep resonant \"jug-o-rum\" from warm, permanent ponds, marshes, and slow rivers thick with vegetation. You meet it in summer, mostly in southern Ontario and around the lower Great Lakes.\n\nBullfrogs are ambush hunters and will take almost anything they can swallow, including other frogs. Development is slow in the north. Tadpoles can spend two or three years in the water before they finish changing into frogs, so a single pond may hold several year-classes at once. Not at risk.",
  "american-toad": "Warty and brown, this common toad turns up in gardens, forests, and fields across most of the province. The best field mark sits behind the eyes: kidney-shaped parotoid glands that stay separate from the crests on the head and never quite touch them. That small detail separates it from Fowler's toad. The spring breeding call is a long, musical trill that can run up to 30 seconds from shallow ponds and ditches.\n\nToads are patient, mostly nocturnal hunters. A single adult can eat thousands of insects, slugs, and worms over a season, which makes one worth having around a garden. They are long-lived for a small animal, sometimes past 10 years. Handle one and you will find out why most predators leave it alone. The parotoid glands release a secretion that irritates eyes and mouth. It will not hurt you through intact skin, but wash your hands after holding a toad and keep your fingers away from your face. Not at risk.",
  "gray-treefrog": "Not colour but climbing gear marks this frog. Large sticky toe pads let it work high into trees and shrubs, where it spends most of its time out of sight. The skin is warty and shifts between grey and green with temperature, activity, and background, so colour alone will not identify it. The giveaway is a flash of bright yellow-orange on the hidden inner thighs when it jumps. The voice is a loud, short, flute-like trill from wooded areas near water.\n\nYou hear far more of these than you see, mostly on spring and summer nights in southern and central Ontario. Cold does not stop it the way it stops most frogs. It floods its tissues with glycerol and tolerates partial freezing, which lets it overwinter under leaf litter and bark. Not at risk.",
  "green-frog": "The banjo player of the marsh. Its call is a single loud \"gunk\" with a plucked, twangy quality, often repeated a few times. Green to bronze on top, it is most easily told from the bullfrog by its dorsolateral ridges, the folds of skin that run down the back but stop short of the groin. The bullfrog has none at all. Size overlaps between the two, so the ridges are the reliable mark.\n\nYou find it in permanent ponds, streams, and the vegetated shallows of lakes through southern and central Ontario, spring into summer. Males hold calling territories and will square off with rivals. Startle one at the water's edge and it gives a sharp squawk as it leaps, a good clue to what just jumped before you got a proper look. Not at risk.",
  "northern-leopard-frog": "Green or brown and covered in rounded dark spots, each ringed with a paler halo. Two prominent light dorsolateral ridges run down the back. This is a frog of open, wet ground, at home in wet meadows, marshes, and the grassy edges of ponds and slow streams, where it hunts by day and often sits well out in the grass away from water. The call is a low snore followed by a few chuckling grunts, easy to miss in a busy spring chorus.\n\nYou can meet it across much of the province from spring through fall. It was once one of the most abundant frogs on the continent, collected in huge numbers for the frog-leg trade and for classroom dissection. Numbers have risen and fallen since, but in Ontario it remains widespread and is not considered at risk.",
  "spring-peeper": "The voice is out of all proportion to the animal. A tiny tan-to-brown frog barely longer than a thumbnail, it is one of the first to call each year, often while nights are still cold. A single peep is a high, clear, rising note. A full chorus of hundreds can top 90 decibels and carry a long way from woodland ponds, marshes, and wet meadows near forest.\n\nSeeing one is harder than hearing it. Look for the dark X-shaped mark on the back that gives the species its name, crucifer, and the small toe pads that let it climb low vegetation. It calls on spring and summer nights and is widespread across Ontario. Once breeding is done it moves into the woods and mostly falls silent, spending the rest of the year in leaf litter hunting small insects. Not at risk.",
  "wood-frog": "Among the first frogs to appear each spring, sometimes over snow, this one heads for temporary woodland pools while they are still cold. It is tan to reddish-brown with a dark \"robber's mask\" running back from each eye, the clearest field mark. The breeding chorus is soft and clucking, more like a group of ducks than a typical frog.\n\nIts cold tolerance is hard to beat. It can freeze solid over winter, heart stopped, then thaw out alive in spring, using glucose in its tissues as an antifreeze. That trick lets it live farther north than any other amphibian in North America, and it is found across Ontario including the far north. Breeding is fast, tied to the brief life of the meltwater pools it uses. After the pools dry, adults spend the rest of the year in moist deciduous and mixed woodland, away from open water. Not at risk.",
  "boreal-chorus-frog": "In the northwest corner of Ontario this small grey-brown frog replaces the very similar western chorus frog. Three broken dark stripes run down the back. By sight the two are nearly impossible to tell apart, so range does most of the work: this is the chorus frog you meet around the Rainy River and Kenora districts. The call is a slow, rising \"crreek,\" like a fingernail dragged over the teeth of a comb.\n\nIt lives in marshes, wet meadows, roadside ditches, and pond edges, and it breeds early. It ranges about as far north as any frog on the continent and may start calling while ice still rings the breeding pond. You hear it through spring and into summer. Not at risk.",
  "mink-frog": "A frog of cool boreal water, easy to mistake for a green frog until you look at the legs or pick one up. The hind legs carry irregular dark blotches rather than the clean crossbands of the green frog. Handle one and the reason for the name is obvious: the skin gives off a musky smell often compared to mink. The call is a rapid, hollow \"cut-cut-cut.\"\n\nIt stays close to lakes, bogs, and slow streams where lily pads are thick, often sitting on the pads themselves. This is a northern species, at home in boreal Ontario and scarce or absent in the south. You meet it mainly in summer. It spends much of its life in or right at the water and does not wander far from it. Not at risk.",
  "pickerel-frog": "Two neat parallel rows of squarish dark spots run down the back, set between the ridges. That squared-off, ranked pattern separates it from the leopard frog, whose spots are rounded and scattered. A flash of bright yellow-orange on the hidden inner thighs shows when it jumps. The call is a low, steady snore, often given underwater and easy to overlook. It favours cool clear streams, springs, and bog margins in southern and central Ontario, and sometimes turns up around cave entrances.\n\nThis one is worth handling with care, mostly for the sake of other animals. Its skin secretions are irritating and can be toxic to other amphibians confined with it, so never bag it alongside other frogs. Those same secretions probably help it deter predators. For people the risk is minor, but wash your hands after handling and keep them away from your eyes. Not at risk.",
  "western-chorus-frog": "A small grey-brown frog with three dark stripes down the back and a pale line along the upper lip. You are far more likely to hear it than see it. The call sounds like a finger dragged slowly over the teeth of a comb, rising as it goes, from grassy marshes, wet meadows, and seasonally flooded fields. It calls early, mostly in spring, in eastern and southern Ontario.\n\nThe Great Lakes / St. Lawrence Lowlands population has dropped sharply, largely through the loss and drainage of the shallow, temporary wetlands it breeds in. It is federally listed as Threatened. The most useful thing anyone can do is leave those wet meadows and flooded fields intact and undrained rather than disturb the frogs. By sight it cannot be reliably separated from the boreal chorus frog; in this part of the province, range points to this species.",
  "fowlers-toad": "In Canada this toad hangs on only along the north shore of Lake Erie, at places like Long Point and Rondeau. It looks much like an American toad, and telling them apart takes a close look at two features. The parotoid glands behind the eyes touch the crests on the head, and each dark blotch on the back holds three or more warts. The call is a short, nasal, unmusical \"waaah,\" nothing like the American toad's long trill. It is active on spring and summer nights and spends the day burrowed into loose sand.\n\nIt lives on sandy beaches, dunes, and open shoreline, habitat that is limited and heavily used by people. The species is Endangered and legally protected. Do not disturb, handle, or collect it, and give calling and breeding areas a wide berth. Its skin secretions are irritating like other toads', but the real reason to keep your hands off is its protected status and how few are left.",
  "eastern-newt": "This species wears two very different looks across its life. As an aquatic adult it is olive-green with a keeled tail; the juvenile stage, the red eft, is bright orange with a row of red spots ringed in black and dry, faintly rough skin. Efts leave the water after the larval stage and wander the moist forest floor for two to seven years before returning to water to mature. You can meet them by day across central and southern Ontario on the Canadian Shield, often out in the open after rain when other salamanders stay hidden. The bold colour is a warning. Skin toxins make the eft distasteful to predators, and those toxins are harmful if swallowed, so wash your hands after any handling and keep them clear of your mouth and eyes.",
  "eastern-red-backed-salamander": "Lift a damp log in deciduous or mixed forest and this is the salamander you are most likely to find under it. It is slender and lungless, breathing through its skin and mouth lining, so it stays where the ground holds moisture. Look for a straight red-orange stripe down the back, though a plain grey 'lead-backed' form is common too, with a mottled salt-and-pepper belly on both. There is no aquatic stage. Eggs are laid on land, often inside a rotting log, and the female curls around them until they hatch as tiny versions of the adult. It is among the most abundant forest vertebrates in eastern North America. It is small and nocturnal, so it is easy to overlook. If you turn a log to look, set it back gently.",
  "blue-spotted-salamander": "Slim and nearly black, this salamander is sprinkled with pale blue flecks along the sides, limbs, and tail. It breeds in spring in vernal pools set in deciduous and mixed forest, and it ranges widely across central and southern Ontario. Most of the year it stays underground or under cover and is rarely seen. The best chance to find one is on the first warm rainy nights of spring, when adults move to water.\n\nThe genetics are unusual. This is one member of a unisexual complex, where all-female polyploid lineages produce eggs but borrow sperm from related Ambystoma species to start development. The result is a tangle of pure and hybrid animals that can be hard to tell apart in the field. For a walker the practical point is simpler. A small dark salamander with blue speckling near a woodland pool in early spring is very likely this one.",
  "mudpuppy": "Large and fully aquatic, this salamander keeps its larval form for life. It never loses its bushy red external gills, which flare when the water is low in oxygen, and it has four toes on each foot. It lives year-round in lakes, rivers, and streams across the Great Lakes and their connecting waters, sheltering under rocks and debris and hunting at night.\n\nWinter anglers sometimes hook one through the ice and are alarmed by it. There is no reason to be. Despite old folklore, it is not venomous and it is completely harmless. If you catch one, unhook it gently and return it to the water unharmed. Mudpuppies are sensitive to pollution, so their presence indicates good water quality.",
  "northern-two-lined-salamander": "Cool running water is where you meet this one. It is slender and yellowish, with two dark lines running back from the eyes toward the tail, and it lives along rocky streams, seeps, and springs in forest across eastern and central Ontario. It seldom strays far from moving water, hiding by day under stones at the edge and emerging at night.\n\nLike other lungless salamanders it has a defence worth knowing about. When a predator grabs its tail, the tail can break off and keep twitching while the salamander slips away, then regrow over time. If you turn stones at a streambank to look for one, set them back as you found them so the small animals sheltering underneath keep their cover.",
  "spotted-salamander": "Two irregular rows of round yellow spots, often orange toward the head, mark this large, glossy-black salamander. It is stout and can grow longer than a hand. For most of the year it lives underground in mature forest and is almost never seen. The exception is a few nights in early spring, when adults travel to fishless vernal pools to breed, sometimes in numbers on the first warm rains.\n\nThe egg masses hold a small surprise. A green alga lives inside the developing embryos in a genuine symbiosis, the alga gaining nutrients and the embryos gaining oxygen from its photosynthesis. It is one of the few known cases of an alga living within a vertebrate's cells. You will find this salamander in southern and central Ontario, most reliably at a breeding pool on a wet night in early spring.",
  "allegheny-mountain-dusky-salamander": "In Canada this salamander lives in one place only. Its entire national range is a single cascading stream in the Niagara Gorge, a Carolinian population that likely holds fewer than 100 adults. That makes it Endangered and legally protected. Do not disturb the habitat or handle the animals.\n\nIt is a slender lungless salamander of spray zones, seeps, and mossy rock faces beside cool forested streams. Look for a straight-edged dorsal band, often with a wavy border, and a pale line from the eye to the jaw. The tail is rounded in cross-section, which helps separate it from the Northern Dusky Salamander that shares the gorge and carries a keeled, flattened tail. Given how few remain and how small the range is, the right thing is to watch from the trail and leave everything as you found it.",
  "four-toed-salamander": "The belly gives this small salamander away. It is enamel-white, boldly marked with black spots, unlike anything else you are likely to turn up in an Ontario bog. Two other marks help: four toes on each hind foot, and a distinct constriction at the base of the tail, where the tail breaks off cleanly if a predator seizes it. It lives in sphagnum bogs and swampy woods with mossy pool margins, scattered across southern and central Ontario.\n\nNesting is often communal. Females lay their eggs together in moss overhanging water, and one clutch may be tended by several females. When the larvae hatch they wriggle free and drop into the pool below to finish growing. It is nocturnal and easy to miss, so most people find it by carefully checking moss at a pool edge and then putting everything back.",
  "jefferson-salamander": "Among the earliest amphibians to move each spring, this salamander migrates to its breeding pools on the first warm rainy nights, sometimes while ice still lingers. That timing puts it on roads before most people are thinking about salamanders, and road crossings are one of the threats it faces. It is Endangered in Ontario and legally protected, so it should not be disturbed or handled.\n\nIt is slender and dark grey-brown, with long toes and faint bluish flecks low on the sides when young. It breeds in fishless vernal pools in upland deciduous forest and spends the rest of the year underground. In Canada it is limited to the Carolinian far south, around Niagara, Hamilton, and Halton. It also overlaps with the unisexual Ambystoma complex, which can make individual animals hard to identify with confidence. If you find one near a pool, keep your distance and let it carry on.",
  "northern-dusky-salamander": "Like its close relative in the same gorge, this salamander survives in Canada at a single stream in the Niagara Gorge, where the Carolinian population numbers only a handful of animals. It is Endangered and protected. The habitat is fragile and the numbers are tiny, so do not turn rocks or handle anything.\n\nIt is a stout lungless salamander of cool rocky seepage streams and springs in shaded ravines. The marks to check are a pale diagonal line from the eye to the angle of the jaw and a keeled, laterally flattened tail. Its hind legs are noticeably stouter than the front, and a disturbed animal can jump well to escape. That keeled tail sets it apart from the Allegheny Mountain Dusky Salamander, which has a rounded tail and shares the same gorge. For a visitor there is nothing to do but look from the path and leave the stream undisturbed.",
  "small-mouthed-salamander": "In Canada you will find this salamander in exactly one place: Pelee Island. It is Endangered and protected, and the whole national range is that single island, so there is no room for disturbance. It lives in moist woodland and fields near the seasonal ponds where it breeds in spring.\n\nThe name fits. The head is notably small and the snout short and blunt, set on a dark body flecked with greyish, lichen-like markings. Those proportions are the easiest way to tell it from other mole salamanders. As with several relatives, it takes part in the Ambystoma unisexual complex here, where all-female lineages use sperm from related species, so some animals are hybrids rather than pure Small-mouthed Salamanders. If you are on Pelee Island near a breeding pond on a wet spring night, watch your step and leave any salamander where it is.",
  "white-perch": "This is a fish that does not belong here. It arrived in the Great Lakes through the canal system and has settled into the warm open water, harbours, and sheltered bays of the lower lakes. Look for a deep, silvery to greenish body with no clear horizontal stripes. That plain flank separates it from the White Bass, which carries thin dark lines along its sides. The two dorsal fins are joined at the base rather than standing apart. You will meet it from spring through fall around Lake Ontario, Lake Erie, and connecting waters like the Bay of Quinte.\n\nThe trouble it causes is real. It eats the eggs of walleye and other fish and competes with native panfish for food and space. Anglers catch plenty of them, but the fish itself is a marker of a changed system, not a welcome addition. Handle it with care. The dorsal spines are sharp and will jab a careless hand.",
  "white-bass": "Roving schools near the surface of warm lakes give this one away. It moves in large groups through open water and big bays, and in spring it runs up rivers to spawn. The body is deep and silvery, marked with several thin dark stripes running lengthwise, one of them carrying all the way to the tail. The lower jaw juts forward, and the two dorsal fins stand clearly apart. Those stripes and separated fins tell it from the White Perch, which is plain-sided with joined dorsals.\n\nIn Ontario it is most at home in the warm lower Great Lakes, Lake St. Clair, Lake Nipissing, and the Bay of Quinte. Spring is the season most anglers know it, when the spawning run concentrates fish in the rivers, but it stays catchable through summer and fall. Watch your hands. The dorsal fin and the gill covers both carry sharp spines.",
  "black-bullhead": "Few fish tolerate the water this one lives in. It holds out in warm, turbid ponds, backwaters, and sluggish streams over soft mud, in places where low oxygen would drive most fish out. The body is dark and chunky, a small catfish with dark chin barbels and a nearly square tail. A pale crescent at the base of the tail is the mark to look for, along with a grey base to the anal fin. That pale bar where the tail meets the body is the quickest field check.\n\nYou will find it across southern and western Ontario, in the Lake Erie, Lake St. Clair, and Lake of the Woods drainages, from spring into fall. It is a bottom feeder that works muddy water by touch and smell. Handle it carefully when you unhook one. The pectoral and dorsal spines are sharp and can drive into a hand that grips too quickly.",
  "green-sunfish": "An oversized mouth sets this sunfish apart from its relatives. The body is stout, blue-green on the sides and flecked with pale spots, with yellow-orange edges to the fins and a black ear flap rimmed in pale. That large mouth lets it take bigger prey than most sunfish, and it shrugs off warm, low-oxygen water that keeps other fish out. It lives in weedy ponds, ditches, and slow creeks.\n\nIts range in southern Ontario is growing, mainly through the Lake Erie and Lake St. Clair drainages. It moves into water other sunfish cannot use, and it can crowd small waters and out-compete native panfish. You will catch it from spring through fall. The gill covers and fins carry sharp spines, so mind your grip when you handle one.",
  "white-crappie": "Murky water is where this crappie has the edge. It handles more turbid conditions than the Black Crappie and holds in warm, sheltered bays, creek mouths, and slow rivers over sand or mud. The body is silvery, marked with faint vertical bars rather than the scattered speckles of its relative. The surest check is the dorsal spines. This one has six, against the seven or eight of the Black Crappie, so a careful count settles which fish is in hand.\n\nIn Ontario it keeps to the warm lower Great Lakes: western Lake Ontario, Lake Erie, Lake St. Clair, and southern Lake Huron. It is a schooling panfish, most active around cover, and you will find it from spring through fall. Nothing about it needs special caution beyond the usual care with a spiny-finned fish.",
  "yellow-bullhead": "The chin gives this catfish away. Its barbels are pale, white or nearly so, while the Brown and Black bullheads both carry dark ones. The body is yellow-brown with a rounded tail. Where the ranges overlap, checking barbel colour is the fastest way to be sure which bullhead you have caught.\n\nIt keeps to warm, weedy, slow water - ponds, marshes, and sluggish creeks with soft bottoms - in southern Ontario, through the Lake Erie and Lake Ontario drainages. Like other bullheads it feeds along the bottom and is active from spring into fall. There is a bit of parenting behind the plain look. After the eggs hatch, the adults stay with the young, guarding the tight school of dark fry until they grow. Handle it with care when unhooking. The pectoral and dorsal spines are sharp and will jab.",
  "northern-sunfish": "This is a fish to admire and leave alone. It is small and colourful, with wavy blue lines across the cheek and a long black ear flap tipped in white or orange. It lives in clear, weedy bays and slow streams with clean substrate, and in Ontario it holds on in only a few waters of the southwest, in parts of the Lake St. Clair, Lake Erie, and Lake Huron drainages.\n\nIt carries the status of special concern under both SARO and COSEWIC, which reflects its narrow range and its dependence on clean, clear water. Anything that clouds or degrades those streams works against it. For years it was treated as the northern form of the Longear Sunfish before being recognized on its own. If you encounter one, note it and let it be. A fish this restricted does not have room to spare.",
  "bloater": "Down in the cold and dark is where this cisco lives. It is a small, silvery deepwater chub with large eyes and a lower jaw that projects past the upper. It holds far below the surface in the deep offshore water of Lake Superior and Lake Nipigon, deeper than the Cisco, or Lake Herring, that it resembles. Because it stays in cold water year round, it is present through all four seasons, though rarely seen by anyone but commercial and research nets.\n\nIt was lost from Lake Ontario, and a reintroduction effort is now under way to bring it back, part of an attempt to rebuild the offshore food web that native trout and salmon depend on. As a forage fish it feeds on plankton and small invertebrates in the depths. It is not one you will catch on a line, but it matters to the systems that hold the fish you do.",
  "pink-salmon": "An accident put this salmon in the Great Lakes. A small release near Thunder Bay in 1956 seeded a population that now reproduces entirely on its own, with no stocking behind it. It is the smallest of the Pacific salmon in these waters, silvery, with large oval spots on the back and across both lobes of the tail. Spawning males develop a pronounced humped back, which is why the species is also called humpback salmon.\n\nMost run on a strict two-year cycle, returning to the tributary streams to spawn in fall after growing in the cool open water of the lakes. It is most common in Lake Superior and Lake Huron and their spawning rivers, and you are most likely to encounter it in summer and fall. It is a naturalized introduction, established and self-sustaining, but not part of the original Great Lakes fauna.",
  "round-whitefish": "Shape is the thing to check with this whitefish. The body is slender and nearly cylindrical, rounder in cross-section than the deep-bodied Lake Whitefish, with a small mouth set beneath a pointed snout. One more mark settles it: a single flap of skin between the nostrils, where the Lake Whitefish has two. It lives in cold, deep water of large lakes and clear rivers.\n\nIn Ontario it turns up in northern lakes and in all the Great Lakes except Lake Erie, whose warmer water does not suit it. It feeds along the bottom on insects, small invertebrates, and fish eggs. Spawning comes in late fall over gravel in shallow water, which is part of why it shows up in spring, fall, and winter records more than in the heat of summer. It is not a fish most anglers target, but it is a clean indicator of the cold, well-oxygenated water it needs.",
  "splake": "This trout came out of a hatchery, not a wild spawning bed. It is a cross of a male Brook Trout and a female Lake Trout, and it looks the part, carrying the wormlike markings of a Brook Trout on its back but a moderately forked tail closer to a Lake Trout's. The blend sits between its two parents in most features.\n\nOntario stocks it into cold, deep inland lakes because it grows quickly and rarely reproduces in the wild, which lets managers put a catchable fish into a lake without it taking over. You will find it in stocked lakes across central and northern Ontario and parts of Georgian Bay, and it stays active through all four seasons, including under the ice. It behaves much like its parent species, holding in cold water and taking baitfish and insects. Treat it as a managed fishery. Its numbers depend on continued stocking.",
  "aurora-trout": "Few fish have come this close to being lost. Native to only two lakes, Whirligig and Whitepine, north of Sudbury, it was wiped out in the wild by acid rain by the late 1960s. It survives today through a captive breeding and reintroduction program, and it is listed as endangered under both SARO and COSEWIC.\n\nIt is a colour form of Brook Trout, but a distinctive one. It lacks the yellow spots and wormlike back markings of a typical brook trout, showing plain sides with red spots reduced or absent. Breeding males take on a deep orange-red belly. It lives in a handful of cold, acid-sensitive lakes on the highlands, water that careful management works to keep habitable. You may find it now in the Lady Evelyn-Smoothwater area and a few designated stocked lakes. Its recovery is real but not secure, tied to lakes that stay vulnerable to acidification.",
  "shortjaw-cisco": "The deepwater ciscoes are a difficult group, and this one takes a close look to name. Its lower jaw sits about even with the upper, or a little shorter, where most ciscoes have a jaw that projects. Counting gill rakers helps confirm it and separates it from the similar chubs it lives among. This is a fish of deep, cold water in large northern lakes. In Ontario it now holds on in Lake Superior and Lake Nipigon, and it is gone from Lakes Huron and Erie where it once occurred. Overfishing and the arrival of invasive species pushed it out of much of its former range, and it is listed as Threatened by both SARO and COSEWIC.\n\nBecause the deepwater ciscoes look so alike and have shifted so much under fishing pressure, even biologists have argued over how to define this one. You are unlikely to meet it without a net set in deep water.",
  "alewife": "A silvery herring of open water, the alewife travels the Great Lakes in mid-water schools. It is deep-bodied and flattened side to side, with a large eye, a belly edged like a saw, and a deeply forked tail. A single dark spot sits just behind the gill cover.\n\nIt came from the Atlantic and spread through the upper Great Lakes, where it has done real damage. Alewife eat the eggs and larvae of native fish, and their arrival reshaped the food web. In some years huge numbers have died off in late spring and washed up along beaches in windrows. It is not a fish anyone sets out to catch. It matters mostly as forage for stocked salmon and trout, and as a problem, and it turns up dead on shorelines often enough that most lakeshore residents know it by sight.",
  "blacknose-dace": "In small cold creeks across southern and central Ontario, this is often the minnow you catch first and most. It is a little brown fish with a dark band that runs from the snout, through the eye, and back to the base of the tail. The snout is pointed and overhangs a small mouth, and there is a tiny barbel at the corner. In spring the males take on orange tints along the lower body and fins.\n\nIt holds in the faster, gravel-bottomed water of headwaters and riffles, where it feeds on drifting insects and bottom life. Common and hardy, it is a familiar catch in a dip net or minnow trap. Its abundance makes it an important food for larger stream fish.",
  "brook-stickleback": "The brook stickleback comes armed. Along its back, ahead of the dorsal fin, stands a row of about five short spines set separately from one another. The body is scaleless and olive to nearly black, and the tail base is very slender. It lives in weedy, cool water: ponds, ditches, marshes, and slow streams.\n\nThe nesting is unusual for such a small fish. In spring the male builds a small barrel-shaped structure out of plant fragments, binding them with a sticky secretion produced by his kidneys. He then guards the eggs and the newly hatched young against anything that comes near. It is too small to interest anglers except as occasional bait, but it is easy to find and easy to recognize once you learn the spines. Handle it gently if you catch one, since the spines lock upright.",
  "central-mudminnow": "Few small fish tolerate bad water as well as the central mudminnow. It is a stubby, dark brown fish with a rounded tail and a dark vertical bar just before the tail. The single soft dorsal fin sits well back on the body. Look for it over mucky bottoms in weedy, oxygen-poor ponds, ditches, marshes, and slow streams, places where most fish cannot live.\n\nIt gets by there with two tricks. It can gulp air at the surface and hold it in its swim bladder, and it burrows down into soft mud when conditions turn against it. That lets it ride out stagnant, low-oxygen water. It is widespread across southern and central Ontario. You will most likely turn it up in a dip net through weeds rather than on a line.",
  "common-shiner": "A deep-bodied silvery minnow of clear streams, the common shiner shows large scales on its sides that stand out plainly and look taller than they are wide. That scale pattern is the quickest way to name it. It lives in gravel-bottomed streams and small rivers, and along the edges of the lakes those streams feed.\n\nIn late spring the males come into colour, with rosy fins and small hard bumps, called tubercles, scattered over the head. They spawn on gravel, sometimes over nests that other stream minnows build. It is one of the more common and easily caught minnows in southern and central Ontario, often taken in bait nets. At a good size it makes a lively baitfish. Out of the water the pink of a breeding male fades quickly, so the colour is best seen in a clear jar or in the shallows.",
  "creek-chub": "Fish almost any Ontario creek and the creek chub will be part of the catch. It is a stout minnow with a large mouth and a dark spot at the front base of the dorsal fin. A small flap-like barbel hides in the groove above the upper lip, which takes a close look to see. Young fish carry a dark stripe down the side and a spot at the base of the tail.\n\nIt holds in creeks and small streams with a mix of pools and gravel riffles. The spawning behaviour stands out. The male builds a low ridge of gravel by picking up stones in his mouth and carrying them one at a time, then guards the nest. Big ones take a hook readily and are sometimes kept for the pan or used as bait. In small water it is often the largest minnow around.",
  "emerald-shiner": "Out over open water in the Great Lakes and large rivers, big schools of this slender minnow feed near the surface. It is translucent and silvery with a faint green sheen along the back, which gives it its name. The eye is large and the mouth opens at the front of a pointed snout. A fairly long anal fin and that slim shape set it apart from the stubbier minnows of the same waters.\n\nIt matters far out of proportion to its size. Emerald shiner are a main forage fish in the Great Lakes, and their surface schools feed walleye, perch, and other predators. Anglers know them as bait and as the thing gulls and larger fish are working when the surface boils. Individually plain and easy to overlook, they are one of the fish that keep the open-water food web running.",
  "fathead-minnow": "If you have bought minnows in Ontario, some were probably fatheads. It is a small, chunky, dull olive fish with a short rounded snout and a dark blotch at the front of the dorsal fin. The lateral line does not run the full length of the body. Breeding males change a good deal. They darken, grow a spongy grey pad across the nape, and roughen the snout with rows of small tubercles.\n\nIt lives in ponds, ditches, and small turbid streams, and puts up with warm, low-oxygen water that would kill most fish. That toughness is why it is one of the standard live baits, sold as the 'tuffy' and, in an orange domestic form, the 'rosy red'. It is raised on fish farms for the trade. In the wild it is widespread and abundant, often one of the last fish holding on in a marginal pond.",
  "gizzard-shad": "Larger than most fish in this group, the gizzard shad is a deep silvery herring of warm, often murky lakes and slow rivers, where it gathers in big schools. The belly is edged like a saw, and a dark shoulder spot sits behind the head. The snout is blunt and overhangs a small mouth set low, and the last ray of the dorsal fin draws out into a long trailing thread. It feeds by filtering plankton and detritus, which it grinds in a muscular, gizzard-like stomach that gives it its name.\n\nIn the lower Great Lakes, Erie, Ontario, and the St. Clair system, it is abundant. Cold is its weakness. Hard winters kill it in large numbers, and dead shad wash up along shorelines in late winter and spring. It is little use to anglers directly, but it is important forage for walleye and other predators.",
  "golden-shiner": "A deep, slab-sided minnow with a golden-bronze cast, the golden shiner is a fish of quiet, weedy water: lakes, ponds, and slow streams. Two features name it. The lateral line curves strongly downward along the lower side rather than running straight, and a scaleless fleshy keel runs along the belly behind the pelvic fins, like the bottom edge of a knife. It has no barbels.\n\nColour ranges from silvery in young fish to a clear brassy gold in adults from tea-stained water. Most run 8 to 18 cm, though some grow larger. It is among the most widely used baitfish in the province and is raised in bait ponds for the trade, which is part of why it turns up in so many waters. In the wild it schools among weeds and feeds on plankton and small invertebrates, and it is common prey for bass, pike, and other predators.",
  "johnny-darter": "On sand and gravel in the shallows, the johnny darter spends its life on the bottom, propped up on its spread pelvic fins between short darting moves. It is small and slender, pale tan, and marked along the side with a scatter of dark spots shaped like W's and X's. It has two separate dorsal fins. Unlike some of its relatives, it wears no bright colour, even in the breeding season.\n\nWatch a stream margin or a clear lake shallow and you may see it hold still, then dart a body length to snap up a small insect, then hold again. When it spawns, the male tends the eggs on the underside of a stone and guards them until they hatch. It is common across southern and central Ontario and easy to overlook. A patient look into clear, shallow water is the usual way to meet it.",
  "logperch": "Largest of Ontario's darters, this is a slender, cigar-shaped fish marked with many narrow dark bars down a yellow-green body. The snout is conical and blunt, almost pig-like, and there are two dorsal fins set close together. It works sand and gravel bottoms in lakes and larger streams, and it earns part of its living by flipping small stones with that snout to reach the insects and other prey sheltering underneath. Look for it over clean bottoms across southern and central Ontario, spring through fall.\n\nLike other darters it lives on the bottom rather than in open water, so you are more likely to see one while wading or snorkelling a clear stream than while fishing for it. It is not at risk. Nothing about it bites or stings, and at this size it is best watched and left where you find it.",
  "longnose-dace": "Fast water suits this small minnow. Streamlined and bottom-hugging, it holds position in riffles and rocky runs where the current is too strong for most fish to stay put. The long fleshy snout overhangs an underslung mouth, and there is a small barbel at the corner. Adults are fairly plain and lack the bold dark side stripe that marks the similar blacknose dace, which is one useful way to tell the two apart.\n\nBesides stream riffles, it turns up along wave-washed rocky lake shores, another place with steady water movement over rock. It ranges widely across Ontario in cold, clean water. You meet it spring through fall, usually by turning stones in a riffle rather than by casting to it. Not at risk, and harmless to handle, though like any small fish it is easily stressed and best returned quickly.",
  "longnose-sucker": "A cold-water sucker of central and northern Ontario, this one ranges farther north than the more familiar white sucker. The body is long and round, with a fleshy snout that overhangs a downward-facing sucker mouth, lips covered in small bumps, and fine scales that crowd together toward the head. It tends to run darker than a white sucker. In spring, spawning fish develop a rosy stripe along the side, which is the easiest time to notice one.\n\nIt lives in cold, clear lakes and streams and feeds along the bottom, drawing in small invertebrates and other material through those thick lips. Look for it spring through fall in the colder waters of the north. Not at risk. Suckers are edible but bony, and telling exactly which sucker you have is a fussy business best done with a good key rather than a glance.",
  "mottled-sculpin": "Broad-headed and tadpole-shaped, this little bottom fish presses itself against the rocks of cold, clear streams and rocky lake shallows. It has no scales, large fan-like pectoral fins that help it hold to the bottom, and two dorsal fins. The colour is a mottled brown with dark saddles across the back, good camouflage on a gravel bottom.\n\nWhere you find sculpins, the water is usually clean and well oxygenated, which is why they are taken as a good sign in trout streams. They share that cold water with trout and belong to the same community. You will not catch one on a line; look instead among stones in a clear riffle, spring through fall. It is not at risk. Handle it gently if at all, since there is a small sharp spine on the gill cover that can prick a careless hand.",
  "rainbow-smelt": "Slender and silvery, with an iridescent wash of purple, green and silver, this small open-water fish carries a trout-like adipose fin and a surprisingly large, toothy mouth for its size. A fresh one smells distinctly of cucumber, which is a reliable field mark. It lives in the cold open water of the Great Lakes and many inland lakes, coming inshore and running up streams to spawn in early spring.\n\nThat spring run is when most people meet it, dipping nets by lantern light for a fish that gathers in large numbers. It was introduced to the upper Great Lakes basin in the early 1900s and spread widely from there. Fishing for it is spring and winter work, through the ice or at the spawning streams. It is not at risk, being an introduced species that is now well established. Smelt are a traditional pan fish, small enough to eat whole after cleaning.",
  "shorthead-redhorse": "Redhorse suckers are hard to tell apart, and the tail is what settles this one. A silvery, large-scaled sucker with a notably small head, the shorthead carries a bright red or orange tail fin that stands out against the rest of the body. Up close, the lower lip has fine grooves and a rear edge that runs nearly straight across, another feature that separates it from its relatives.\n\nIt lives in clean lakes and in moderate to fast rivers, feeding along gravel and sand bottoms. It is one of the more common redhorse in Ontario rivers, found across the southern and central parts of the province from spring through fall. Not at risk. Like other suckers it is edible but full of fine bones, and telling redhorse species apart takes a careful look at lips and fins rather than a first impression.",
  "trout-perch": "The name records an oddity. This small fish carries a soft, trout-like adipose fin on its back and, at the same time, weak spines in its fins of the sort you expect from a perch, a combination that once puzzled the people who named it. The body is semi-translucent with rows of dark spots along the back and side, a large blunt head, and a tapering rear.\n\nIt lives over sand and gravel bottoms in lakes and larger rivers, keeping to deep water by day and moving into the shallows to feed after dark. That night habit means it usually goes unseen. It ranges across Ontario and is active spring through fall. Not at risk. It is small and harmless, of little interest to anglers except as forage for the larger fish that eat it.",
  "goldfish": "The goldfish in the wild is rarely gold. Released from aquariums and garden ponds, feral fish revert to a plain olive-bronze or grey, and they look much like a small carp. The body is carp-like but lacks the barbels a carp has, and the long dorsal fin is stiffened by a serrated spine. Feral populations have taken hold in warm, weedy, often stagnant water, including sheltered harbours such as Hamilton Harbour, where they can also hybridize with carp.\n\nThis is an introduced and invasive species, present spring through fall in southern Ontario ponds and Great Lakes harbours. A clear rule comes with it. Never release pet goldfish or any live fish into the wild. It is illegal in Ontario, and released fish can establish themselves and damage native waters. If you no longer want an aquarium fish, rehome it rather than dumping it in a pond.",
  "quillback": "At a glance this looks like a carp, deep-bodied and high-backed and silvery, but it is a sucker and the differences show on a closer look. There are no barbels around the mouth, which is small and sucker-like, and the long dorsal fin has its front rays drawn out into a tall quill that gives the fish its name and a sail-like profile.\n\nIt keeps to slow pools of large rivers and lakes, over soft bottoms where it feeds. In Ontario it belongs to the lower Great Lakes and the larger southern rivers, and you meet it spring through fall. Not at risk. The carp resemblance means it is often misidentified, so check for barbels: a carp has them, a quillback does not. Like the other suckers it is bony eating.",
  "rainbow-darter": "Few Ontario fish carry as much colour as a breeding male of this small darter. In spring he turns out in alternating bars of blue-green and orange across the body and fins, while females and out-of-season fish stay a modest mottled brown. Like other darters it has two dorsal fins and lives on the bottom, holding among gravel and cobble in the riffles of clear, fast streams.\n\nIn Ontario its range is narrow, centred on the southwestern streams of the Grand and Thames watersheds. Being a bottom fish of clean, moving water, it is found by turning stones or watching a riffle rather than by fishing. It is best seen in spring, when the males are at their brightest. Not at risk in Ontario, though a fish tied to clear streams depends on those streams staying clean. It is tiny and harmless, worth a careful look and nothing more.",
  "silver-redhorse": "Where the shorthead redhorse shows a red tail, this one shows grey. Heavy-bodied and large-scaled, the silver redhorse is the thickest-lipped of Ontario's common redhorse, with full, deeply grooved lips ending in a rounded rear lobe. The tail runs grey to slate rather than red, and that colour is the quickest way to separate it from the red-tailed shorthead.\n\nIt favours the deeper pools of medium to large rivers and the lakes connected to them, feeding along the bottom through those heavy lips. Found in the larger rivers of southern and central Ontario, spring through fall. Not at risk. Redhorse are notoriously alike, and getting to species means looking closely at the lips and tail rather than trusting a first impression. Edible but bony, like the rest of the suckers.",
  "largemouth-bass": "A greenish fish with a bold dark stripe running the length of its side, the largemouth is named for a jaw that extends back past the eye. Its two dorsal fins are nearly separated, which helps tell it from the smallmouth bass. It holds in warm, weedy water, in lakes and slow backwaters with plenty of cover to ambush from. That oversized mouth is not just for show, since it can take prey up to about a third of its own length.\n\nThis is a warm-water gamefish and a popular one in southern and central Ontario, active by day and most catchable through the warmer months. Not at risk. Anglers should check the current fishing regulations before keeping any, since seasons and limits apply and vary by zone. It fights hard for its size, which is much of the reason people fish for it.",
  "northern-pike": "Long and torpedo-shaped, the pike hangs motionless in the weeds and lets prey come to it. When something passes within reach it bursts out and seizes it, sometimes taking fish nearly half its own length. The body is dark green with rows of light, bean-shaped spots. To tell it from a muskie, check the cheeks and gill covers: on a pike the cheeks are fully scaled but the gill covers only partway. You will find pike in weedy bays, along shallow shorelines, and in slow rivers across the whole province, and they stay active through the ice.\n\nA caught pike is worth handling with respect. The mouth holds rows of needle-sharp teeth, so grip firmly behind the head and use pliers to free a hook.",
  "smallmouth-bass": "Bronze-sided and built for current, the smallmouth holds in clear lakes and cool rivers where gravel and boulders break the flow. The body carries faint vertical bars and the eye is often tinged red. A quick way to separate it from a largemouth: the jaw ends at or before the eye, not behind it. For its size it fights harder than almost anything in fresh water, running deep and throwing itself clear of the surface.\n\nLook for it around rocky points, mid-lake shoals, and river runs from spring through fall. It favours cooler, cleaner water than the largemouth and shares much of southern and central Ontario with it. Crayfish and small fish make up much of what it eats.",
  "walleye": "The glassy, reflective eye is the giveaway, and it is also the clue to how this fish lives. A light-gathering layer at the back of the eye, the tapetum lucidum, lets the walleye see and feed in murky water and in the low light of dusk and dawn. That is when it moves up onto reefs, drop-offs, and shoreline flats to hunt. During bright midday it usually sits deeper or in stained water. Look for the white tip on the lower lobe of the tail, and note the plain spiny dorsal fin, which separates it from the closely related sauger.\n\nWalleye are found across the province and stay catchable through the ice. Handling one calls for some care: the jaws hold sharp teeth, the first dorsal fin is spiny, and the edges of the gill covers are sharp enough to cut.",
  "yellow-perch": "Few fish are as easy to recognize as the yellow perch. The body is golden-yellow, crossed by six to eight dark vertical bars, with lower fins washed in orange. Perch travel in schools, sometimes large ones, moving through weed beds and open water alike. Where you find one you usually find many, which is part of why they are among the most popular fish on the Ontario table.\n\nThey feed by day on insects, small fish, and invertebrates, and they keep biting through winter, making them a mainstay of the ice fishery. You will meet them in lakes and slow rivers across the province. No special handling worries here, though the spiny first dorsal fin is worth a moment's attention when you unhook one.",
  "muskellunge": "Ontario's largest member of the pike family is hard to catch. Anglers call it the fish of 10,000 casts, a nod to how rarely one commits to a lure. The pattern is the reverse of a pike: dark bars or spots on a lighter background. To confirm an identification, check that only the lower half of the cheek and gill cover is scaled, and count six or more sensory pores under each side of the jaw. Muskie hold near weedlines, rocky bars, and deeper structure in large lakes and rivers, and they are most active through summer and into fall.\n\nThe best water for them is the bigger systems: the Kawarthas, the Great Lakes, and northern rivers. These are powerful fish with a mouthful of teeth. Use a heavy leader, and cradle a large one in the water to release it rather than lifting it clear.",
  "sauger": "At a glance the sauger looks like a small walleye, and the two are close relatives. Look closer and the differences show: rows of distinct dark spots run through the spiny dorsal fin, the tail has no white tip, and dark saddle-shaped blotches mark the sides. Sauger handle turbid, current-swept water better than walleye and are more at home in big, murky rivers and the lakes connected to them. Where the two overlap they sometimes cross, producing a hybrid known as the saugeye.\n\nLike the walleye, it feeds most in low light, at dawn and dusk. You will find it in larger rivers and lakes, including parts of the Great Lakes and northern watersheds, mostly through spring, fall, and winter. The same handling cautions apply: sharp teeth, a spiny first dorsal fin, and sharp-edged gill covers.",
  "black-crappie": "Silvery and flecked with irregular black speckling, the black crappie earns its old nickname 'calico bass' from that mottled pattern. It is a schooling fish, roaming in loose groups around weed beds, sunken wood, and other cover in warm lakes and slow rivers. To separate it from the white crappie, count the dorsal spines: seven or eight here, five or six on the white.\n\nCrappie feed most actively in the low light of morning and evening, and they gather in numbers, so finding one often means finding a school. In Ontario they are largely a southern and central fish. They stay active under the ice, which makes them a common winter catch. The flesh is well regarded on the table, and there is nothing about handling them that calls for particular care.",
  "bluegill": "Round and deep-bodied, the bluegill is one of the small sunfish that crowd weedy, warm water. Two marks pin it down: a solid dark ear-flap with no coloured margin, and a dark blotch at the rear base of the soft dorsal fin. It lives among vegetation in lakes and ponds, rarely straying far from cover.\n\nIn late spring and early summer the males clear saucer-shaped nests on the bottom, often packed side by side in colonies, and guard them hard against anything that comes near. That is when they are easiest to catch and most fun on light tackle. Bluegill are a southern and central Ontario fish, active by day through the warm months. They take small baits readily and are a common first catch for children learning to fish.",
  "brown-bullhead": "A small catfish of soft, weedy bottoms, the brown bullhead feels its way through the dark rather than relying on sight. The whisker-like barbels around its mouth are lined with taste buds, and it uses them to find food in murky water and at night, when it is most active. Recognize it by the squarish tail, the dark chin barbels, and the saw-toothed rear edge on the pectoral spine. It lives in warm lakes and slow rivers across the province, usually over mud or among plants.\n\nOne warning worth taking seriously: the stiff spines at the front of the pectoral and dorsal fins are sharp and can drive a painful wound into a careless hand. Grip a bullhead carefully, keeping fingers clear of those spines, when you unhook one.",
  "pumpkinseed": "Among the most colourful of the small sunfish, the pumpkinseed is marked by an ear-flap tipped with a bright red-orange spot and wavy blue lines running across the cheek. It keeps to weedy shallows in lakes, ponds, and quiet river margins, seldom far from cover. Snails are a staple of its diet, and it is well equipped for them: a set of crushing pharyngeal jaws deep in the throat lets it break open hard shells that other fish leave alone.\n\nPumpkinseed are widespread through southern and central Ontario and active by day in the warm months. They are small, willing biters, easy to catch from a dock, and often the first fish a young angler brings in. Handling them poses no particular problem.",
  "rock-bass": "The red eye gives this one its common name, 'redeye,' and it is the fastest way to know it. Chunky and rather plain next to the brighter sunfish, the rock bass wears rows of dark spots along a brassy body. Count the anal spines to be sure: five or six here, against three in the true sunfish like the bluegill and pumpkinseed. As the name suggests, it holds close to rock - boulders, ledges, and sunken logs along the margins of lakes and rivers.\n\nIt is widespread through southern and central Ontario and active by day from spring into fall. Rock bass take a bait quickly and often aggressively, which makes them easy company on the water. There is nothing about handling them to watch out for beyond the usual spiny fins.",
  "brook-trout": "Despite the name, this is not a true trout but a char, and it belongs to the coldest, cleanest water Ontario has. The back carries wormlike markings, called vermiculations, the sides are dotted with red spots each ringed by a pale blue halo, and the lower fins show a clean white leading edge. Brook trout need cold, well-oxygenated water and do not tolerate warming or pollution, so their presence is a fair sign of a healthy stream.\n\nYou will find them in headwaters, spring-fed creeks, and clear northern lakes, where lake-dwelling fish grow larger than the stream fish. They are most reachable in spring and fall, when the water is cool. Because they depend so completely on cold, clean habitat, brook trout populations are a useful measure of a watershed's condition, and many local waters are managed with that in mind.",
  "chinook-salmon": "The biggest of the Pacific salmon, Chinook were stocked into the Great Lakes to hold alewife numbers in check and now anchor the region's salmon fishery. They spend most of the year in open water, feeding and growing fast, before running up rivers in fall to spawn. Most fall between 60 and 100 cm, though some push past 120.\n\nTo tell a Chinook apart, look inside the mouth: the gums and mouth interior are black, and small black spots mark both lobes of the tail. Summer trolling brings them within reach of anglers, and fall crowds them into tributary mouths. Like other Pacific salmon, they die after spawning, so a run is a one-way trip. These are introduced fish, not native to the lakes, and their presence is maintained through stocking. That makes local numbers depend heavily on how many young fish are released each year and on the state of the forage base they feed on.",
  "cisco": "Also called lake herring, the cisco is a slender, silvery fish of open, cool water, moving in schools through the pelagic zone. The deeply forked tail and the small fleshy adipose fin mark it as a member of the salmon family. A useful field mark is the lower jaw, which projects slightly beyond the upper. That is the reverse of the lake whitefish, whose snout overhangs its mouth.\n\nCisco matter to the food web out of proportion to their size. Lake trout and walleye rely on them as forage, and where cisco are healthy the predators tend to be too. They are also a fine fish on the table and take well to smoking. Look for them in fall and winter, when they gather to spawn and come within reach of ice anglers on many cold inland lakes as well as the Great Lakes. Numbers have slipped in some waters, so local regulations are worth checking before you keep a catch.",
  "lake-trout": "Lake trout are a char, not a true trout, and they live where few other large fish can: the deep, cold, oxygen-rich basins of northern lakes. A dark body scattered with pale cream spots and a deeply forked tail set them apart, and unlike some of their relatives they carry no pink or red spots.\n\nThey grow slowly and live a long time, sometimes more than 40 years, which makes a population slow to recover once it is knocked back. Ontario holds a large share of the world's lake trout lakes, most of them on the Canadian Shield, along with populations in the Great Lakes. You can find them through spring and summer and again through the ice in winter. Because they hold in deep water for much of the year, catching them often means fishing well below the surface. A big fish is an old fish, and removing many of them thins a slow-growing stock.",
  "lake-whitefish": "A bottom feeder of deep, cold water, the lake whitefish makes its living on invertebrates it picks from the lake floor. The small mouth set under an overhanging snout is the giveaway, paired with a silvery body and a deeply forked tail. That overhanging snout separates it from the cisco, which has a slightly projecting lower jaw. Both belong to the whitefish group within the salmon family.\n\nThis is one of the more valued fish in Ontario waters, both commercially and on the plate, with firm pale flesh that many rank among the best of the cold-water fishes. Whitefish spend the open-water season in deep parts of the Great Lakes and large inland lakes, then move shallower to spawn over gravel in late fall. That fall movement, and the winter that follows, is when most anglers meet them, often through the ice. They feed quietly near bottom, so light rigs and small baits tend to work best.",
  "rainbow-trout": "Lake-run rainbow trout go by a second name, steelhead, and the two are the same fish living different lives. In the lakes they turn chrome-bright and hard to separate from other silvery fish, but a pink-to-red band along the side and small black spots scattered across the body and tail give them away.\n\nThey spend much of the year in cold Great Lakes water and ascend tributaries to spawn, mostly in spring, with fish staging in rivers through fall and winter as well. On their spawning runs, steelhead will leap small falls to reach the water they are headed for, which makes river mouths and lower rapids good places to watch for them. Rainbows were brought here from western North America and have naturalized, breeding on their own in many streams rather than depending entirely on stocking. Unlike the Pacific salmon, they do not necessarily die after spawning and may run more than once.",
  "brown-trout": "Brought over from Europe, the brown trout has settled into cool streams and the nearshore zones of the Great Lakes. Its spots tell the story: black and red-orange, each ringed with a pale halo, over a squarish tail that carries few spots or none. That pattern separates it from the more heavily spotted rainbow and from the char, which show light spots on a dark ground.\n\nBrowns have a reputation for being hard to catch, and it is earned. Large individuals become mostly nocturnal and grow wary, holding to cover and feeding in the low light of dawn and dusk. Look for them in spring and fall, in streams and along shorelines where the water stays cool. They will take a range of baits and lures, but the bigger fish reward patience and a quiet approach. As an introduced species they compete with native trout in some waters, so local rules on size and season are worth knowing.",
  "coho-salmon": "Coho are the smaller, scrappier of the two Pacific salmon stocked into the Great Lakes, known among anglers for aggressive, hard-charging strikes. The mouth sorts them from the similar Chinook: a coho has a white or pale gumline with a dark tongue, and black spots on the upper lobe of the tail only, not both.\n\nThey spend the growing season in open water and run up tributaries to spawn, mostly in fall. Summer and fall are the seasons to look for them, first out in the lake and later staging near river mouths. Like the other Pacific salmon here, coho die after spawning, so a run is the end of the line for those fish. They were introduced and are not native, so their numbers lean on stocking and on the health of the baitfish they feed on. Where the forage base is strong, the fish come back heavier the following season.",
  "atlantic-salmon": "Unlike the Pacific salmon in the Great Lakes, the Atlantic salmon is a native fish here, or was. The Lake Ontario stock was gone by the late 1800s, lost as its rivers were dammed, logged, and overfished, and the fish you might encounter today are the work of an ongoing effort to bring it back. Look for black spots shaped like Xs or crosses above the lateral line, a slim caudal peduncle where the body narrows to the tail, and a tail that carries no spots.\n\nIt uses the cold lakes and rivers of the Lake Ontario system, running into tributaries to spawn in fall, with fish also present in spring. This is a species at risk in the plainest sense: its original population was wiped out. The reintroduction is still finding its footing and depends on years of stocking and habitat repair. If you catch one, handle it gently and know the local rules, which may require release.",
  "common-carp": "A heavy-bodied import from Eurasia, the common carp thrives in warm, shallow, often murky water over soft, muddy bottoms. Two barbels hang at each corner of the mouth, the scales are large, and a long dorsal fin runs down the back with a hardened, serrated spine at its front edge. Adults commonly reach 40 to 80 cm, and some pass a metre.\n\nCarp feed by rooting through bottom sediment, and that habit is the trouble with them. Stirring up mud clouds the water and uproots plants, degrading habitat that native fish and waterfowl depend on. That is why the species is treated as invasive across southern Ontario. They are most active through spring and summer, often in the low light of dawn and dusk, and are easiest to find in shallow bays and slow river stretches. Strong and long-lived, they are caught by anglers who fish for them on purpose, and their stubborn fight makes them a target in their own right.",
  "freshwater-drum": "Freshwater drum spend their time on the bottoms of large lakes and rivers, over sand and gravel, where they crush snails and other hard-shelled prey. The body has a high, arched back and a rounded tail, and the lateral line runs unusually far, continuing out onto the tail fin itself.\n\nThe name comes from a sound the males make. By vibrating muscles against the swim bladder they produce a low grunting or drumming that carries through the water and, at times, up to a boat. Look for them in summer and fall, most active in the low light around dawn and dusk. They are common in the Great Lakes and the large rivers of southern Ontario, and they are frequently caught by anglers fishing bottom rigs for other species. Drum are edible, though not widely sought as table fish. The heavy throat teeth that let them handle snails also make them one of the few fish here that feed heavily on molluscs.",
  "round-goby": "Small, bottom-hugging, and out of place, the round goby is an invader from the Black and Caspian seas that reached the Great Lakes in the ballast water of ships. It settles on rocky and gravel bottoms, where it competes with and displaces native bottom fish and eats their eggs. Two field marks stand out: the pelvic fins are fused into a single round suction disc on the belly, and there is a distinct black spot at the rear of the first dorsal fin. Most are only 7 to 15 cm long.\n\nYou are most likely to meet one in summer, often when it grabs a bait meant for something else. This is a fish to know and to handle correctly. It is illegal in Ontario to use or transport round goby as bait, and moving it to new water spreads the problem. If you catch one, do not put it back and do not carry it away alive. Its spread has already reshaped parts of the Great Lakes food web.",
  "white-sucker": "The white sucker feeds with its mouth turned down toward the bottom, working over the floor of streams and lakes across most of the province. That ventral mouth, with its thick fleshy lips, is the clearest mark, set on a rounded body whose scales grow noticeably smaller toward the head. It is one of the most widespread fish in Ontario and turns up in water of many kinds, from small creeks to large lakes.\n\nSpring is when suckers announce themselves, gathering into large runs up streams to spawn, sometimes in numbers that fill a riffle. They matter as food and as bait. Young suckers feed many predators, and the species is a common baitfish. On the table they are bony but perfectly edible, and were long a staple of spring fisheries. If you use them as bait, use them only where the law allows, and never move live fish between waters.",
  "bowfin": "The bowfin is one of Ontario's older fish lineages, the sole survivor of a group that mostly died out long ago. You find it in warm, weedy backwaters and marshy lake bays where the water sits still and the oxygen runs low. It gets by there by gulping air at the surface, using a modified swim bladder as a crude lung. That habit lets it hang on in water that would kill most other fish.\n\nLook for a long, low dorsal fin with no spines running most of the back, and a rounded tail. Males carry a dark eyespot near the base of the tail. The body is thick and almost cylindrical, olive to brown. Anglers meet it in spring and summer, most often around dawn and dusk. It takes bait readily and fights hard.\n\nHandle it with care. The jaws are strong and the teeth are sharp, so keep your fingers clear when unhooking. It is native and common through southern and central Ontario, and not at risk.",
  "burbot": "Ontario's only freshwater member of the cod family lives deep and cold. Burbot hold in the depths of large lakes and big rivers, moving shallower mainly in winter. The body is long and mottled brown, almost eel-like toward the tail, with a single barbel under the chin and long soft dorsal and anal fins that run back to meet the rounded tail.\n\nIt goes by several names, ling and eelpout among them. Unlike most fish here, it spawns under the ice in midwinter, sometimes gathering in writhing balls of intertwined fish over sand or gravel. It feeds at night, taking smaller fish and prey off the bottom.\n\nBecause it lives deep, most people who catch one are ice fishing or working a bait near bottom in cold water. The flesh is firm and good to eat. Burbot are widespread in the Great Lakes and cold inland lakes across the province, and carry no special protection.",
  "channel-catfish": "A deeply forked tail sets the channel catfish apart from Ontario's other catfish, which have square or rounded tails. The anal fin is rounded, and young fish carry scattered dark spots that often fade with age. Colour runs from slate to olive over a pale belly, and four pairs of barbels frame the mouth.\n\nIt favours large warm rivers and lakes with some current and deep holes to rest in. The skin is covered in taste buds, so the fish reads its surroundings by taste over nearly its whole body, an asset for finding food in murky water after dark. Spring and summer bring it within reach of anglers, and it feeds mostly at night.\n\nTake care when handling one. The stiff spines at the front of the pectoral and dorsal fins can drive a painful puncture, so grip the fish behind them. In Ontario it turns up in southern rivers and the lower Great Lakes, including Lake St. Clair and the Grand River. It is good eating and not at risk.",
  "lake-sturgeon": "Few Ontario fish live as long or grow as large as the lake sturgeon. It can pass a century and top 100 kilograms, though fish that size are old and now rare. Rows of bony plates called scutes run along the body, the tail is shark-like with an upper lobe longer than the lower, and four barbels hang ahead of a toothless, tube-like sucker mouth that extends to vacuum prey off the bottom.\n\nIt lives in the deep pools of large rivers and lakes, feeding on the bottom, mostly at night. Slow to mature and slow to reproduce, it was heavily overfished, and its populations have not recovered evenly. Status ranges from threatened to endangered depending on the population.\n\nIf you catch one, the law protects it. Release it unharmed, and mind the scutes, which are sharp. Seasons are closed in many waters, so check the regulations before fishing where sturgeon live. You are most likely to meet it in the Great Lakes and large northern rivers.",
  "longnose-gar": "Nothing else in Ontario looks like a longnose gar. The jaw is drawn out into a long, narrow beak lined with teeth, more than twice the length of the head, and the body is armoured in diamond-shaped ganoid scales that interlock like plate. It is an ancient design that has changed little over millions of years.\n\nThe fish hangs near the surface of warm, weedy bays and slow rivers, often basking almost motionless, then striking sideways to seize small fish in that toothy jaw. Unlike most of the fish it lives among, it is active by day. Spring and summer are when you are most likely to see one.\n\nTwo cautions matter. The teeth are sharp, so handle the head carefully. More important, the roe is toxic to people. Never eat gar eggs. The flesh is eaten by some, but the eggs are dangerous, so be certain of exactly what you are keeping. Longnose gar live in southern Ontario, including the Great Lakes and the Kawarthas, and are not at risk.",
  "sea-lamprey": "The sea lamprey is not a fish in the usual sense. It is jawless and eel-like, with no paired fins, and it feeds by fastening its round, tooth-filled sucker mouth to the side of a larger fish and rasping through the skin to take blood and body fluids. Seven small gill pores run along each side behind the eye.\n\nNative to the Atlantic, it reached the upper Great Lakes through shipping canals and did heavy damage to lake trout and other stocks. Adults ascend tributary streams in spring to spawn, then die. Control programs, mainly targeted lampricides applied to spawning streams, now hold numbers down, though the work never stops.\n\nThis is an invasive parasite. Where control programs apply, do not move live lampreys between waters or return them alive. If you find one attached to a fish, it is doing exactly what it does for a living. The species is present throughout the Great Lakes and their tributaries.",
  "american-eel": "Every American eel in Ontario began life in the Atlantic. They hatch in the Sargasso Sea and drift thousands of kilometres as tiny larvae before entering fresh water, where they may live for years or decades before returning to the sea to spawn once and die. It is the reverse of the salmon's journey.\n\nThe body is snake-like, with a single long dorsal fin that runs into the tail, smooth and slippery skin over tiny embedded scales, and a lower jaw that projects past the upper. It moves and feeds at night. In Ontario it belongs to the Lake Ontario, St. Lawrence, and Ottawa River systems.\n\nThe species is endangered and protected here. It is illegal to fish for, keep, or harm American eel. Numbers fell sharply after dams and other barriers cut off its migration routes. If you catch one by accident, release it unharmed.",
  "eastern-red-cedar": "The name misleads. This is a juniper, not a true cedar. Look for a narrow, dense evergreen carrying two kinds of leaf at once: sharp, awl-like needles on young or vigorous growth, and tight scale-like leaves pressed flat against older twigs. Female trees are hung with pale blue cones that look like berries and wear a waxy bloom. The bark is reddish-brown and peels off in thin, shreddy strips.\n\nYou meet it on dry limestone ground in southern and eastern Ontario, on alvars, in old fields, along fencerows, and on rocky shores. It holds its foliage year round. Cedar waxwings and other birds eat the berry-like cones and carry the seeds off, which is much of how it moves into open land. It tolerates thin, poor soil that many trees cannot, so it often marks bare rock and abandoned pasture where little else has taken hold.",
  "scots-pine": "An orange-barked pine on sandy ground is usually this one. The colour shows best on the upper trunk and larger limbs, where the bark flakes off in thin plates and can be picked out from a distance. Needles come two to a bundle, short, blue-green, and often twisted.\n\nIt is not native. Brought from Eurasia and planted heavily for reforestation and as a Christmas tree crop, it has since seeded itself out of plantations into old fields, roadsides, and open sandy sites across southern and central Ontario. You find it standing year round. Much of the planted stock was poorly formed, so escaped trees are often crooked or forked rather than straight. It counts as naturalized here, established well enough to reproduce on its own, though it does not spread with the force of some other introductions.",
  "canada-yew": "Unlike most conifers, this one stays low and sprawling, rarely more than knee to chest height, its branches leaning and straggling through the shade. The flat needles resemble hemlock but run longer, sit in two ranks along the twig, stay green on both sides, and end in a fine point. Females carry seeds set singly in a soft, red, cup-shaped aril.\n\nIt grows in cool, moist forest, in ravines, and on shaded rocky slopes, mostly through central and northern Ontario with scattered stands in the south. Deer feed on it hard through winter, and heavy browsing has thinned or erased it from much of the settled south.\n\nTreat it as poisonous. The foliage and seeds contain taxine alkaloids and can be dangerous or fatal to people and livestock if eaten. The red flesh of the aril is not the toxic part, but the seed inside it is, and there is no safe reason to test the difference.",
  "red-spruce": "Spruces are told apart by small details, and this one rewards a close look. The needles are four-sided, yellow-green, shiny, and sharp at the tip. Rolled between the fingers they feel square. The twigs are finely hairy, and the reddish-brown cones fall not long after they release their seed, which helps separate it from black spruce, a tree that hangs onto its small cones for years.\n\nOntario sits at the western edge of its range. It grows in the east and centre of the province, in cool, moist upland forest and mixed woods, often on shallow soil over rock. Where its range meets that of black spruce the two can hybridize, and the in-between trees can be genuinely hard to name. It is native and secure here, if never as common as the spruces of the boreal north.",
  "pitch-pine": "Count the needles. Three to a bundle sets this pine apart from the two-needle pines that are far more common in Ontario. They are stiff and often a little twisted. The cones are egg-shaped and armed with a small prickle on each scale, and the trunk frequently carries odd tufts of needles sprouting straight from the bark.\n\nIt keeps to hard ground: dry sandy flats and rocky ridges on poor, acidic soil. In the province it is local and uncommon, found in the east on the Frontenac Axis and in the Ottawa Valley, near the northern edge of its range. Fire shapes its life. Where many pines are killed outright, this one can push new shoots from its trunk and root collar after a burn, one of the few eastern pines able to do so. Fire suppression, by closing in the open ground it depends on, works against it over time.",
  "alternate-leaf-dogwood": "Every other dogwood in Ontario holds its leaves in opposite pairs. This one sets them singly, alternately along the twig, though they bunch near the branch tips closely enough to look whorled at a glance. The surer sign is the shape of the whole plant. Branches spread out in flat, tiered layers, one above the next, which is why it is also called pagoda dogwood.\n\nLook for it along forest edges, in the understory, and in clearings in moist woods through southern and central Ontario, growing as a large shrub or small tree. In early summer it carries flat-topped clusters of small white flowers. These give way to blue-black berries held on bright red stalks, a combination that birds find quickly. It is native and common enough within its range. The layered branching and alternate leaves make it easy to name once you have seen them.",
  "american-mountain-ash": "Compound leaves with eleven to seventeen toothed leaflets give this small tree a look that recalls a true ash, though the two are not related. Flat clusters of white flowers open in spring and set into tight bunches of bright orange-red berries by late summer. The bark stays smooth and grey.\n\nIt favours cool, moist ground, growing through northern and central Ontario in damp woods, along rocky shores, and at the edges of bogs. The berries are its most useful field mark in the cold months, since the clusters cling on well into winter. Grosbeaks, waxwings, and ruffed grouse strip them over the season. The fruit is sour and firm and not something to eat raw off the tree, though the birds that depend on it are reason enough to leave it be. A close relative, showy mountain-ash, overlaps with it and looks much alike.",
  "bebb-willow": "Willows are a hard group, and this is one of the more common ones you will run into almost anywhere in Ontario, from southern fields to the boreal north. It grows as a tall shrub or small tree in moist open woods, clearings, fens, and along streams. The dull green leaves are widest above the middle, a little wrinkled on top, with veins that stand out clearly.\n\nIts best-known feature is not really the plant's own doing. A fungus attacks the stems and the wood responds by forming deep, diamond-shaped grooves down the trunk. Carvers value this figured wood, and diamond willow walking sticks and crafts are cut from stems marked this way. Even without the fungus it turns up often enough to be worth learning. Naming willows to species usually takes patience and more than one feature, so read the leaf shape and the setting together rather than trusting any single mark.",
  "bitternut-hickory": "Check the buds in winter and this hickory names itself. They are bright sulphur-yellow, unlike the buds of any other tree in its range, and they hold that colour through the cold months. In leaf it carries compound leaves with seven to nine leaflets. The nut is round, wrapped in a thin husk, and as the name warns, bitter.\n\nIt grows in moist ground, in lowland and slope forests through southern and south-central Ontario, where it is the commonest hickory and the one that ranges furthest north. The nuts are not worth gathering to eat; the kernel is bitter enough that even squirrels tend to take them late, after better food is gone. The wood is hard and strong like that of other hickories. For identification, though, nothing beats those yellow buds, which settle the question at a glance long after the leaves and nuts have dropped.",
  "black-locust": "A pair of short, sharp spines at the base of each twig is the quickest way to place this tree. The leaves are compound, with seven to nineteen rounded leaflets that carry no teeth. The bark is thick and deeply furrowed into a network of interlacing ridges. In June it hangs out clusters of fragrant white flowers shaped like pea blossoms, followed by flat brown pods that rattle on the tree into winter.\n\nIt is not native to Ontario. Brought north from the central Appalachians, it has naturalized across the south and spreads aggressively by seed and by root suckers, often forming thickets on old fields, roadsides, and sandy disturbed ground. The wood is hard and strongly rot-resistant, long valued for fence posts that outlast most others. Treat the plant itself with care. The bark, leaves, and seeds are toxic if eaten, so it is not a tree to sample or to plant where livestock graze.",
  "black-willow": "Most willows in Ontario are shrubs. This is the one that grows into a proper tree, and the largest native willow in Canada. It often leans out over the water on several trunks at once, with dark, deeply furrowed bark and a broad, untidy crown. The leaves are narrow, finely toothed, and green on both surfaces rather than pale beneath.\n\nWater is where you find it, along riverbanks, lakeshores, and other wet ground through southern Ontario. Its roots knit into the soil and hold banks against the current, so it does real work stabilizing shorelines where it grows. The wood is soft and light and not much used for lumber. Like other willows the twigs are brittle and snap off cleanly, and broken pieces washed downstream can root and start new trees, which is part of how it spreads along a river system over time.",
  "downy-serviceberry": "Among the first trees to flower in spring, often while the woods are still bare. The blossoms come in drooping clusters, each with five narrow white petals, opening before the leaves have fully unfolded. The young leaves are wrapped in soft white down that wears off as they mature. The bark is smooth and grey with darker vertical streaks.\n\nLook for it in dry woods, on forest edges, in clearings, and on rocky slopes through southern and central Ontario, growing as a large shrub or small tree. The berries ripen in early summer, which is where the name juneberry comes from. They are sweet and edible, among the more reliable wild fruits in the province, taken readily by people and by birds. As with any wild food, be sure of the plant before eating, though serviceberries are distinctive in flower and fruit. The related smooth serviceberry overlaps and is likewise edible.",
  "manitoba-maple": "Among Ontario's maples this is the odd one out. Its leaves are compound, split into three to seven toothed leaflets, so a quick glance can suggest ash rather than maple. The paired samaras settle the question, hanging in dense clusters through the season. Twigs are green and often coated with a waxy bloom. You meet it along southern and central rivers and floodplains, and just as often on disturbed ground and road edges, where its speed and cold tolerance let it get ahead of slower trees. It is the fastest-growing and most cold-hardy of the native maples, which is also why it turns up where nobody planted it.\n\nThe seeds contain hypoglycin A, a compound linked to seasonal pasture myopathy, a muscle disease that is often fatal in horses. Keep horses off pastures where the seeds have fallen, especially in autumn and after storms have brought down a heavy drop.",
  "mountain-maple": "A small, often shrubby maple of the shaded understory. It rarely gains much height, growing as a multi-stemmed thicket in moist ravines and along streambanks beneath taller trees. The leaves are modest, three-lobed and coarsely toothed. The feature that identifies it in the field is the flower and seed cluster. Where nearly every other maple lets its samaras hang, this one holds its spikes upright. That detail separates it from every other maple in the province.\n\nIt grows as understory across most of Ontario, seldom noticed until you learn to read the standing seed spikes. In autumn the leaves can turn red and orange, and because the tree stays low, that colour sits close to eye level rather than overhead. Look for it in cool, shaded woods where the soil holds moisture through the summer.",
  "pin-cherry": "Look for shiny reddish-brown bark marked with pale horizontal lines, the lenticels that run across it. The leaves are narrow and pointed, and the fruit is a small, sour red cherry carried on a long stalk in loose clusters. It is one of the first trees to seed in after a fire or a clearcut, which earns it the name fire cherry. That habit makes it a short-lived pioneer, quick to hold open ground while slower forest trees establish. It grows across the whole province.\n\nThe cherries are very sour and are used mostly in jelly rather than eaten off the tree. Be careful with the rest of the plant. The leaves, twigs and pits contain cyanide compounds and are toxic to livestock, and wilted leaves are especially dangerous to grazing animals. The thin flesh of the ripe cherry is the only part people use.",
  "pussy-willow": "In late winter the twigs produce silky grey catkins that push out before the leaves, and for most people that is the whole reason to know this willow. Those soft catkins are among the earliest signs of spring in Ontario wetlands, and they give bees an early source of pollen when little else is flowering.\n\nIt grows as a large shrub or small tree in wet meadows, at marsh edges, along streams and in damp thickets. The leaves come later, elliptic, dark green above and pale beneath, with wavy or shallowly toothed margins. Twigs are reddish-brown. You will find it through southern and central Ontario wherever the ground stays wet. Cut branches are often brought indoors in early spring, and the catkins hold their shape for a long time once they dry.",
  "striped-maple": "The bark gives this one away. It is smooth and green, streaked with vertical white lines, unlike any other tree in an Ontario hardwood forest. The leaves are large and three-lobed, broad enough that the shape is often compared to a goose's foot. It stays small, living out its life in the shade of taller hardwoods rather than reaching the canopy.\n\nIt is also called moosewood, because moose and deer browse its twigs and bark through winter when other food is scarce. Look for it in cool, shaded hardwood and mixed forest in central and eastern Ontario. In autumn the leaves turn a clear yellow. Because it grows in deep shade and seldom gets large, it is easy to walk past until you notice the striped bark at close range.",
  "american-sycamore": "Few trees are easier to name from a distance. The bark flakes away in patches and leaves the upper trunk and limbs smooth and pale, mottled cream and white, and that shows plainly across a winter floodplain. The leaves are very large and maple-like, and the seeds ride in round balls that hang singly on long stalks and stay on through winter.\n\nIt has the largest trunk diameter of any hardwood in eastern North America, and old riverside specimens can be enormous. In Ontario it keeps to the Carolinian southwest, lining rivers, floodplains and moist bottomlands. Winter is the season to look, when the leaves are down and the white upper bark stands out against bare woods. It needs consistent moisture, so it follows the water and does not stray far up onto dry ground.",
  "black-maple": "This is a near-twin of sugar maple, and telling the two apart takes attention. The leaf edges tend to droop, the lobes usually number three rather than five, and the undersides feel fuzzy to the fingertip. The bark runs darker. It is a close relative of sugar maple and is tapped for syrup in exactly the same way, with sap of similar quality. In Ontario it is a tree of rich, moist floodplain and bottomland soils, limited to the southwest.\n\nWhere the two grow together they hybridize, which blurs the line further, so an intermediate-looking tree may be exactly that. Autumn colour runs to yellow and orange. If you are trying to confirm it, check the drooping leaf margins and the fuzzy undersides together rather than leaning on any single feature.",
  "black-oak": "A tree of dry, sandy ground, this oak defines the black oak savannas at places like Ojibway Prairie and the Rice Lake plains. The leaves are glossy with bristle-tipped lobes, and their shape varies a good deal even on one tree, which can make identification difficult. Two features help. The inner bark is orange-yellow, and the winter buds are grey and noticeably hairy.\n\nIt grows on the southern Ontario sand plains, in oak savanna and open woods on dry sandy soils. That orange inner bark was once the source of a yellow dye called quercitron. Like other members of the red oak group it carries bristle tips on its lobes, so look to the buds and inner bark to separate it from its relatives. In autumn the leaves colour and the open savanna is easy to read.",
  "grey-birch": "Poor, dry ground is where this birch makes its living, seeding into old fields, burns and rocky clearings that few trees will take. The bark is chalky white to grey, but unlike white birch it does not peel away in papery sheets, and a dark triangular chevron sits below each branch where it meets the trunk. The leaves are long-pointed triangles that tremble in the wind like aspen.\n\nIt is a short-lived pioneer, quick to colonize disturbed and infertile ground and then give way as richer growth catches up. In Ontario it grows mainly in the east. The non-peeling bark and the dark chevrons are the quickest way to separate it from white birch at a glance.",
  "pin-oak": "The branch pattern is worth learning. Lower branches droop toward the ground, middle branches stand out horizontal, and upper branches angle up. The leaves are deeply cut with bristle tips and wide U-shaped sinuses, and the acorns are small. Dead lower branches tend to stay attached rather than dropping, studding the trunk with short spurs, and that pin-covered look is what names the tree.\n\nIt grows on poorly drained clay flats and floodplain edges. In Ontario it is native only to the wet clay plains near Lake Erie in the far south. Autumn brings deep red to russet colour. It is widely planted in towns well outside its native range, so a pin oak on a city street tells you nothing about local wild distribution.",
  "rock-elm": "This elm carries wood that is hard, heavy and shock resistant. That toughness once made it a wood of choice for hockey sticks, wagon wheel hubs and shipbuilding. In the field it is a tall tree with a narrow, cylindrical crown. Its leaves are smaller and smoother than those of other elms but keep the uneven, lopsided base typical of the family. A good clue is the twigs, which often develop irregular corky ridges or wings.\n\nIt grows in dry to moist upland woods, on ridges and limestone slopes through southern and central Ontario. Like other elms it is vulnerable to Dutch elm disease, which has thinned its numbers over the past century, so a large mature rock elm is less common now than it once was.",
  "showy-mountain-ash": "Two mountain-ashes grow in the north, and they are easy to confuse. This one has fewer and broader leaflets that end in a shorter point, and its berries are larger and a deeper red than those of the American mountain-ash. The winter buds are dark and sticky, another useful check. The two often share the same ground, so compare leaflet shape and fruit size when both are present.\n\nLook for it on rocky shores, cliffs and in cool moist woods, often near water, through northern Ontario and the upper Great Lakes region. The fruit clusters hang into winter and feed birds well after the leaves have dropped. The berries are bitter and astringent when raw, and are used cooked, in jelly, rather than eaten straight off the tree.",
  "slippery-elm": "The inner bark tells you what this is. Peel a strip and it comes away reddish and slimy, and that slime is what named the tree. People once chewed it or brewed it as a remedy for a sore throat. Above ground the leaves are the other giveaway: large, lopsided at the base, and rough enough on top to feel like fine sandpaper. The buds carry rusty hairs through the winter.\n\nLook for it in moist lowland woods, on slopes and floodplains across southern Ontario, where it mixes into the hardwood forest. It is native and not considered at risk, but it lives under the same shadow as white elm. Dutch elm disease reaches it too, and large old trees are uncommon for that reason. Most you meet are young or mid-sized, tucked in the understorey or along a damp edge. Spring, summer and fall all give you something to check, though the rough leaves and slimy bark make it easiest to place in the leaf-on months.",
  "swamp-white-oak": "Flip a leaf and the identification is half done. Dark green above, the underside is pale to nearly white and slightly fuzzy, which is where the name bicolor comes from. The acorns hang on long stalks, longer than most oaks bother with, and the young branches often peel and curl in a ragged way that catches the eye once the leaves are gone.\n\nThis is an oak of wet ground. It grows in bottomlands, along swamp edges and on floodplains, tolerating flooded soil that would kill most of its relatives. In Ontario it keeps to the wet woods of the far south. It is native and secure. Summer and fall are the seasons to know it, the two-toned leaves through the warm months and the stalked acorns dropping later. If you are standing in a soggy southern woodlot and find an oak that seems comfortable with wet feet, this is the likely candidate.",
  "american-chestnut": "Once a common forest tree, it is now something you almost have to hunt for. An introduced fungal blight swept through in the twentieth century and killed nearly every mature tree. What survives in Ontario is a few hundred, scattered through the Carolinian southwest, and it is listed as Endangered. Often all you find is a cluster of sprouts rising from an old blight-killed stump, growing for a while before the blight finds them again.\n\nThe leaves are long and narrow with large curved teeth, each tooth ending in a small bristle. The nuts sit inside spiny green burs. The spines on those burs are sharp and will jab bare hands, so handle fallen ones with care. Summer and fall are when the tree shows itself, in leaf and then in fruit. Report any you find to a conservation authority, given how few are left.",
  "blue-ash": "Among Ontario's ashes this one stands apart, and the twigs prove it. They are four-angled and squarish in cross-section, often with corky ridges running along the corners, a feature no other native ash shares. The compound leaves carry seven to eleven sharply toothed leaflets. The name comes from a blue dye: crush and soak the inner bark and it colours the water blue.\n\nIt grows on dry limestone soils, alvars, rocky slopes and open woods, mostly in the extreme south around Pelee Island and the Lake Erie shore. It is listed as Special Concern. The emerald ash borer has gutted ash populations across the province, and while blue ash shows more resistance than the ashes it grows beside, it is not immune, and its small range keeps it vulnerable. Check the square twigs in spring or after leaf-fall. Once you have felt the four hard angles, the tree is hard to mistake for anything else.",
  "chinquapin-oak": "The leaves look more like a chestnut than an oak, narrow and edged with many pointed teeth, but lacking the bristle tips a chestnut carries. The acorns are among the sweeter ones in the oak family. The bark is grey and flaky.\n\nThis is one of Ontario's rarest native oaks. It grows on thin soils over limestone, on dry slopes, alvars and rocky bluffs, including Manitoulin Island and the Niagara region. It is native but provincially rare, so a wild tree is a genuine find rather than something you stumble on. Fall is the season it announces itself, when the acorns drop. The acorns are edible and less bitter than most, but the tree's scarcity is reason enough to leave them for the wildlife that depend on them. If you are botanizing on limestone in the right corner of the province, chestnut-like leaves on an oak are the thing to watch for.",
  "common-hoptree": "More shrub than tree, it stays small and often grows low and many-stemmed near the water. The leaves split into three glossy leaflets that smell strongly when you crush them. Small greenish-white flower clusters give way to flat, round, papery winged seeds that look a good deal like elm fruit. Those winged fruits were once used in place of hops in brewing, which is how the tree got its name.\n\nIts home is the sandy and gravelly shorelines, dunes and open woods of the lower Great Lakes, along Lake Erie and Lake St. Clair, including Point Pelee and Pelee Island. It is listed as Threatened in Ontario, tied to a narrow band of shoreline habitat that is easily lost to development and erosion. Summer and fall are when to look, first for the scented three-part leaves, then for the clusters of round paper wings that hang on into autumn.",
  "cucumber-tree": "This is Canada's only native magnolia, and in Ontario it hangs on in just a few small wild populations in the southwest, around the Norfolk and Niagara areas. It is endangered. The large oval leaves run from about 10 to 25 cm long. The flowers are greenish-yellow and tulip-shaped, sitting high in the canopy in late spring where they are easy to walk under and never notice. The name comes from the unripe fruit, green and knobby and shaped a little like a small cucumber.\n\nLook for it in rich, moist deciduous forest in the Carolinian zone, the same warm pocket of the province that holds most of Ontario's rarities. Spring and summer give you the best chance, first the high flowers, then the odd green fruit. Given how few wild trees remain, leave any you find undisturbed.",
  "flowering-dogwood": "The spring display is a bit of a trick of the eye. What look like four broad white petals are actually bracts, modified leaves, arranged around a small cluster of the true flowers, which are tiny and greenish in the centre. The leaves are oval with veins that curve to follow the leaf edge, a reliable dogwood trait, and they turn deep red in fall alongside clusters of red berries. The bark breaks into small blocky plates.\n\nIt is a small understorey tree of rich deciduous forests and shaded slopes in the Carolinian zone of extreme southwestern Ontario, and it is endangered here. Dogwood anthracnose, a fungal disease, has killed many trees and is a large part of why. Spring and fall are the seasons it stands out, the white bracts early and the red leaves and berries late. In between it fades into the green understorey and is easy to pass by.",
  "kentucky-coffee-tree": "The leaves are among the largest you will see on a native tree here, twice-compound and sometimes close to a metre long, divided into many small leaflets. For much of the year, though, the tree stands bare. Its stout blunt twigs leaf out late and drop early, giving it a gaunt, dead-looking profile in the off-season. Female trees carry thick, flat, reddish-brown pods.\n\nSettlers roasted the seeds as a coffee substitute, and that is where the name comes from. This matters for safety. The raw pods and seeds contain a toxic alkaloid and should not be eaten. Roasting is what made them usable, and the raw material is not food. In Ontario only a few natural stands survive, in the floodplain and bottomland woods of the southwest, and it is listed as Threatened. Summer and fall are the seasons to find it, by the huge leaves and, on female trees, the hanging pods.",
  "pignut-hickory": "A hickory of dry ground, it holds to upland woods and ridges in the Carolinian southwest, where it reaches the northern edge of its range. The compound leaves usually carry five leaflets. The bark stays tight, with shallow interlacing ridges rather than the shaggy, peeling strips of some relatives, so it will not be confused with shagbark. The nut is pear-shaped inside a thin husk.\n\nThe husk is the detail worth knowing. On this tree it splits only partway down, unlike the husks of other hickories that open fully and free the nut. Fall is the season to sort it out, when the nuts are on the ground and the husk behaviour is plain to see. It is native and not at risk, though in Ontario you will only meet it in the far southwest. Anywhere north of the Carolinian zone, you are almost certainly looking at a different hickory.",
  "balsam-fir": "The needles settle the identification. They are flat and blunt, and if you turn one over it shows two pale white bands down the underside. The bark is smooth and grey and studded with resin blisters that pop stickily if you press them. The cones stand upright on the branch rather than hanging, and they break apart in place instead of dropping whole, so you rarely find an intact fir cone on the ground.\n\nThis is the fragrant Christmas tree many people picture. The resin from those bark blisters, known as Canada balsam, was once used as an optical cement for mounting lenses and microscope slides. It grows in cool moist woods and boreal mixedwoods through central and northern Ontario. Summer and winter both suit a visit, since it holds its needles year-round. The scent alone, released when you crush a needle between your fingers, is often enough to name it.",
  "black-spruce": "A tree of cold wet ground, it thrives in bogs and muskeg where the soil is waterlogged and poor, conditions most trees refuse. The crown is narrow and spire-like, often with a distinct tuft at the top, and the needles are short and stiff. The cones are small and sit in clusters near the top of the tree.\n\nIt has a useful trick for spreading in soft ground. Lower branches that sag down and touch the wet moss can take root and send up new shoots, a process called layering, so a single tree can slowly form a small ring of clones around itself. This helps it hold ground in the boreal peatlands of northern Ontario, where it is one of the defining trees. Summer and winter are both fair times to know it, since like other spruces it stays evergreen. On a northern bog, the skinny black spire with the tufted top is usually this.",
  "eastern-hemlock": "Cool ravines and shaded north slopes are where this tree does best. The needles are short and flat, set unevenly along the twig, with two pale lines on the underside. The topmost shoot, the leader, bends over in a soft droop that helps separate it from spruce and fir at a distance. It grows slowly and tolerates deep shade better than almost any other Ontario conifer, so seedlings can wait years in the understorey before a gap in the canopy opens. Individual trees may live for centuries.\n\nYou find it through central and southern Ontario, usually in moist forest where the soil stays cool. It holds its needles year-round and reads much the same in summer and winter. One thing to watch: the hemlock woolly adelgid, a small sap-feeding insect from elsewhere, has reached southern Ontario and threatens stands there. Infested trees show white woolly flecks at the base of the needles.",
  "eastern-white-cedar": "Foliage is flat and scale-like, arranged in fan-shaped sprays rather than as separate needles. The bark is reddish and shreddy, peeling in long vertical strips. It grows in swamps and moist ground over limestone, and also clings to dry cliff faces where little else takes hold. Some of the oldest trees in Ontario are stunted cedars rooted in cracks along the Niagara Escarpment, gnarled and slow-growing, far older than their small size suggests.\n\nAn open-grown cedar keeps a dense, conical shape and stays green through winter. It tolerates calcareous soils that many trees avoid. Deer browse it heavily where they are common, which can strip the lower foliage and thin regeneration in southern stands. The light, rot-resistant wood has long been used for fence posts, shingles and canoe ribs. You meet it across central and southern Ontario, most often near water or on rock.",
  "eastern-white-pine": "The provincial tree of Ontario, and for a long time the centre of its timber trade. Soft blue-green needles come in bundles of five, a reliable mark since no other native pine here carries five to a bundle. The cones are long and slender, often curved and sticky with resin. Older trees take on a tiered, wind-swept crown that rises above the surrounding canopy and can be picked out from a distance.\n\nIt grows across much of Ontario on sandy soils, rocky shores and in mixed forest, reaching great height and age on good sites. Logging through the 1800s removed most of the original old-growth pine, so the largest trees today are uncommon. It keeps its needles year-round. Widespread and easy to find, it remains one of the more familiar trees in the central and Great Lakes-St. Lawrence forests.",
  "jack-pine": "Poor ground suits this pine. It takes hold on sandy flats and bare rock in the boreal forest where richer soils are absent, often forming even-aged stands after fire. The needles are short, twisted and paired, and the small cones curve and stay clamped tight to the branch, sometimes for years.\n\nThose cones are serotinous, sealed with resin that only the heat of a fire melts, so the seed is released onto freshly burned, open ground. This ties the tree closely to the fire cycle of the north. It is smaller and scrubbier than most Ontario pines, seldom straight or tall on the poorest sites. You find it through boreal and northern Ontario, green year-round. Where fire has been suppressed, jack pine can struggle to regenerate, since without heat many cones simply stay shut.",
  "red-pine": "Two stiff needles to a bundle, and a clean snap when you bend one, set this apart from white pine at a glance. The bark breaks into broad reddish plates, which is where the name comes from. It favours dry sandy and rocky sites and often grows in pure stands.\n\nMuch of what you see in Ontario was planted. Red pine was used heavily in reforestation through the last century, and rows of it still mark old plantation blocks on sandy ground. Left to grow it forms a tall, straight trunk with a fairly open crown, and it holds its needles through winter. It occurs across central and northern Ontario, and the wood goes to poles, posts and lumber. On natural sites it, like jack pine, is adapted to fire and regenerates well on exposed mineral soil.",
  "tamarack": "Unusual among conifers, this one drops all its needles every autumn. The soft needles grow in tufted clusters along the twig, green through summer, then turn clear gold in fall before dropping, leaving the tree bare and grey through winter. That gold is a good way to pick tamarack out of a boreal wetland in October.\n\nIt grows in cold bogs, fens and wet lowlands where the ground stays saturated and acidic, conditions few trees tolerate. The tough roots were once used to sew birchbark canoes and the wood for boat knees. You find it across boreal and northern Ontario wetlands, less often to the south. Because it needs open, wet, sunny ground, it is easily shaded out on drier sites. In spring the new needles come in a soft pale green.",
  "white-spruce": "Roll a needle between your fingers and it turns easily, since spruce needles are four-sided rather than flat like those of fir or hemlock. Crushed foliage gives off a sharp, skunky smell that some find unpleasant, one way to separate it from black spruce. It grows on well-drained boreal soils, along riverbanks and through mixed northern forest.\n\nThis is a major timber tree in Ontario, cut for pulpwood and lumber across the north. The crown is dense and conical, green year-round, and the slim cones hang down before dropping whole. It handles cold and a range of soils, part of why it is so widespread in the boreal. You will meet it throughout boreal and northern Ontario, often mixed with poplar, birch and balsam fir.",
  "american-basswood": "Leaves are the quick tell: large, heart-shaped and noticeably lopsided at the base, with toothed edges. In early summer it hangs clusters of small, fragrant yellow flowers from a narrow leafy bract, and the scent draws bees in numbers. It is one of the better nectar trees in the southern hardwood forest, and basswood honey is well known.\n\nIt grows in rich, moist woods and on lower slopes through southern and central Ontario. The wood is soft, pale and even-grained, which makes it a favourite for hand carving. Basswood often sprouts from the base, so you find several trunks rising in a ring from one old stump. It loses its leaves in fall. A useful mid-canopy tree to learn, since the crooked leaf shape is hard to confuse once you have seen it.",
  "american-beech": "Smooth blue-grey bark sets this tree apart in a hardwood forest, staying tight and unbroken even on old trunks. The leaves are oval and toothed, the winter buds long, pointed and shaped like thin cigars. Its nuts are small and three-sided, held in a bristly husk, and feed bears, deer and many birds. It grows in rich, moist forest, often alongside sugar maple, through central and southern Ontario. Beech holds some of its pale dead leaves through winter, which makes young trees easy to spot in a bare wood.\n\nThere is a real concern here. Beech bark disease, caused by an introduced insect together with a fungus, is killing beech across much of the province. Affected trunks develop pocked, cankered bark, and many mature trees are declining or dead. Where the disease has passed through, you often see dense thickets of root sprouts in place of the old canopy trees.",
  "balsam-poplar": "In spring the buds give it away. They are sticky with fragrant resin that scents the air on a warm day, a smell once gathered as \"balm of Gilead.\" The leaves are oval and pointed, dark green above and whitish beneath, on furrowed grey bark. It grows fast in moist bottomlands, along rivers, on floodplains and in boreal clearings, and it is one of the northernmost hardwoods on the continent.\n\nBees collect the bud resin and work it into propolis for their hives. You find it across Ontario but especially in the north, often in wet ground where poplar and willow mix. Like other poplars it is short-lived and quick to colonize open, disturbed sites, and the leaves turn yellow before dropping in fall. The resinous buds and their scent are the most reliable way to name it, since the leaves alone can be mistaken for other poplars.",
  "black-cherry": "Mature bark looks like dark, curled cornflakes, broken into small scaly plates, and that is the surest field mark on an older trunk. The leaves are narrow, glossy and finely toothed, and in summer the tree hangs long drooping clusters of small dark cherries that birds strip quickly. It grows along woodland edges, in old fields and mixed forest through southern and central Ontario, often seeded in by birds. The reddish-brown wood is valued for furniture and cabinetmaking and is among the more sought-after hardwoods in the province.\n\nA caution worth taking seriously: the wilted leaves, twigs and seeds contain cyanogenic compounds and are toxic if eaten. This matters most for livestock, which can be poisoned by browsing branches or eating fallen, wilting leaves. Wildlife eat the fruit pulp, but the stones and foliage should not be treated as safe to chew or swallow.",
  "eastern-cottonwood": "Big triangular leaves with toothed edges, set on flattened stalks that let them flutter in the least wind, mark this streamside poplar. The bark is grey and deeply furrowed on old trunks, and the tree grows quickly to a large size. It keeps to floodplains, sandy riverbanks and lakeshores in southern Ontario, where its roots reach reliable water.\n\nIn early summer the female trees release quantities of cottony, wind-borne seed that drifts and piles along shorelines and roadsides, which is where the name comes from. It is one of the fastest-growing native trees here and among the largest hardwoods of the south. The soft wood is weak and the branches shed readily, so old cottonwoods drop limbs. The leaves turn yellow before falling. On a warm windy day the steady rustle of the leaves is a good clue even before you reach the tree.",
  "green-ash": "Green Ash grows where the ground stays wet: floodplains, riverbanks and low forests that flood in spring. The leaves are compound and set opposite each other on the twig, green on both surfaces, which separates it from white ash with its pale undersides. Its keys are single-winged and narrower than a white ash key. For decades it was planted along streets because it grew fast and took hard conditions.\n\nThe emerald ash borer has undone that. The beetle, introduced from Asia, tunnels under the bark and kills nearly every ash it reaches, and green ash has been hit hard across the southern Ontario lowlands. Standing dead ash are now common along many rivers. Fall is the season to notice it, when the leaves turn yellow. Native. If you have one on your property, expect it to need attention; untreated ash in infested areas rarely survive long.",
  "ironwood": "Ironwood is a small understory tree of dry upland woods, growing in the shade beneath oaks and maples. The bark is its best mark: grey-brown and shredding into narrow vertical strips that lift at the ends. Leaves look much like a birch, oval and doubly toothed. In late summer it hangs clusters of papery sacs that resemble hops, each holding a seed, which is where the other name, hop-hornbeam, comes from.\n\nThe wood is true to the name. It is among the hardest and heaviest of any Ontario native tree, dense enough that it was long used for tool handles and fence posts. The tree itself is easy to overlook, rarely growing tall or straight, but it is common through southern and central Ontario. Look for it in summer and fall. Native.",
  "largetooth-aspen": "The coarse teeth along the leaf edge give this aspen its name. Where trembling aspen has fine teeth, largetooth aspen carries large, rounded ones, only a handful to a side. In spring the new leaves open silvery and downy, a soft grey flush that fades to green as they mature. It leafs out noticeably later than trembling aspen, which is a reliable way to separate the two in early spring before the leaves reach full size.\n\nIt favours dry to moist upland soils, often sandy ground, old clearings and forest edges through central and southern Ontario. Like other aspens it grows quickly, does not live especially long, and is cut for pulpwood. The soft buds and bark feed grouse, deer and beaver. In fall the leaves turn yellow, sometimes all at once across a stand. Native.",
  "norway-maple": "This is a European maple planted across southern Ontario cities for its dense shade and easy growth, and it has since walked out of yards into nearby woods. The five-lobed leaves are broad, wider than they are long, and close enough to sugar maple to fool people. The quickest check is the leaf stalk: snap it, and Norway maple leaks a drop of milky white sap where sugar maple runs clear. The keys hang in wide pairs set almost in a straight line, nearly horizontal.\n\nWhere it invades native forest the trouble is the shade. It casts such heavy shade that native tree seedlings and spring wildflowers cannot get the light they need beneath it, and over time it replaces them. It is not recommended for planting, and pulling young ones before they seed is the usual advice. Introduced and invasive. Easiest to notice in spring flower and fall colour.",
  "red-maple": "Few trees are as willing to grow anywhere as red maple. It turns up in wet swamps and on dry ridges alike, which makes habitat a poor way to identify it. The name points to the better marks. The twigs, buds, flowers and fall leaves all run red, and in early spring it is one of the first trees to bloom, tinting whole swamps a dull red before other trees leaf out. The leaves have three to five lobes with toothed, jagged edges, shallower than a sugar maple's and rougher along the margin. Fall colour ranges from yellow to deep scarlet, often on the same tree. It is common through central and southern Ontario and easy to find. Native.",
  "red-oak": "Red oak is a tall, straight tree of well-drained uplands and rocky ridges through central and southern Ontario. Its leaves have pointed lobes, each tipped with a fine bristle, which separates the red oak group from the white oaks with their rounded lobes. On older trunks the grey bark carries smooth, shiny ridges that run down the tree like ski trails, a mark worth learning.\n\nThe acorns take two full years to ripen, so a tree carries two sizes at once. When they drop they are a heavy food for deer, wild turkeys, blue jays and squirrels, and a good acorn year feeds a lot of animals. It grows fairly fast for an oak and makes strong, useful wood. Fall is when the leaves turn, often a deep russet red. Native and common.",
  "silver-maple": "Silver maple belongs to wet ground: floodplains, riverbanks and bottomlands that flood in spring. Its leaves are deeply cut into five narrow lobes and pale silvery-white underneath, so when the wind turns them the whole crown flashes silver. It grows fast, faster than most native maples, and pays for it with weak wood. The branches are brittle and shed readily in ice and windstorms, which is worth keeping in mind with an old one leaning over a house or driveway.\n\nThe seeds are large for a maple and ripen in late spring, unusually early among our trees. Look for it through southern Ontario along rivers and in low, wet woods, often growing with green ash and elm. Fall colour is an ordinary yellow rather than anything bright. Native.",
  "sugar-maple": "This is the maple most people picture, the one on the flag and the source of Ontario syrup. It grows in rich, well-drained upland forests and can reach a good size, forming much of the hardwood canopy across central and southern Ontario. The leaf has five lobes with smooth-edged sinuses between them and no teeth along the margin, which is the cleanest way to separate it from red and silver maple.\n\nIn fall it turns a strong orange-red, and a hillside of sugar maple is much of what draws people out to look at autumn colour. The paired winged keys spin down in fall. Sap is drawn in late winter, when cold nights and mild days get it running, then boiled down to syrup. It is common and long-lived. Native.",
  "trembling-aspen": "The trick is in the leaf stalk. Trembling aspen hangs round leaves on flattened stems, so the least breeze sets them shaking and the whole tree seems to shiver. The bark is smooth and greenish-white, sometimes mistaken for birch, but it does not peel in papery sheets. It is a pioneer, first onto burns, clearings, roadsides and any bare disturbed ground, growing fast in full sun.\n\nMuch of its spread is underground. It sends up suckers from the roots, so a single tree can become a whole grove of genetically identical stems that leaf out and turn gold together in fall. It is one of the most widely distributed trees in North America and grows across all of Ontario. Individual trunks are short-lived, but the clone can persist a long time. Native and common.",
  "white-elm": "White elm, also called American elm, once lined streets across Ontario in tall arching rows before Dutch elm disease took most of them. The classic shape is a vase: a single trunk spreading into arching limbs that meet overhead. Leaves are oval and doubly toothed, with a base that is clearly lopsided, one side meeting the stalk lower than the other. It grows in moist bottomlands, floodplains and old fields through southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe disease, a fungus spread by bark beetles, still kills most elms before they reach full size, so large old ones are now uncommon and worth noting when you find them. Younger elms remain common in hedgerows and second growth, since the trees seed and grow before the disease reaches them. Fall colour is yellow. Native.",
  "paper-birch": "The white bark is the giveaway. Paper birch peels in thin horizontal papery sheets, chalky white on the outside and orange-tan beneath, and it holds that colour through winter, which is one of the good seasons to pick it out in a bare wood. The leaves are small, oval and doubly toothed. It is a pioneer of the boreal and mixed forest, quick onto burns and clearings, and grows across all of Ontario.\n\nThe waterproof bark was traditionally used for canoes, containers and roofing, and it still lights a fire when damp. One caution: stripping bark from a living tree wounds it and can let in rot, so take bark from fallen trees rather than standing ones. Fall colour is yellow. Native.",
  "white-ash": "White ash is a tree of rich, well-drained upland forest, the drier counterpart to green ash. The leaves are compound and opposite, made of several leaflets, and pale, almost whitish, on the underside, which gives the tree its name. On older trunks the grey bark is ridged in a tight, even diamond pattern that is easy to learn. The keys are single-winged and paddle-shaped.\n\nAcross Ontario its future is grim. The emerald ash borer, an introduced beetle, tunnels beneath the bark and kills nearly every ash it infests, and white ash populations are collapsing along with the other ashes. Standing dead trees are now a common sight where the borer has passed through. Fall colour can be a deep purple, unusual among our trees. Native. An untreated ash in an infested area is unlikely to survive.",
  "yellow-birch": "Scratch a twig and you get a faint smell of wintergreen. That, together with the shiny bronze-yellow bark peeling in thin papery curls, is the quickest way to name this birch. It grows tall for the group, reaching 18 to 25 metres, and stands as the largest native birch in Ontario. Look for it in cool, moist mixed forests and along stream edges, mostly through the central and northern parts of the province.\n\nSeedlings often start life on a rotting log or mossy stump, and mature trees sometimes end up perched on stilt-like roots where the nurse log has since rotted away. The wood is hard and even-grained, which is why it turns up in furniture and flooring. Fall and winter are good times to pick it out: the leaves turn a clear yellow, and once they drop the peeling bark stays legible against the snow.",
  "american-hornbeam": "Run a hand over the trunk and it feels like muscle under skin. The smooth blue-grey bark ripples in sinewy, fluted ridges, which is where the names musclewood and blue-beech come from. This is a small understory tree, usually 5 to 10 metres, living in the shade of rich hardwood forests and along streambanks in southern and central Ontario. The leaves are doubly toothed and birch-like, and by late summer the seeds hang in loose leafy clusters, each nutlet set against a small three-pointed wing.\n\nDespite its modest size, the wood is among the hardest of any native tree here, dense enough to dull tools and rarely worth cutting for lumber. It seldom dominates a stand. Expect it as scattered individuals below the canopy, holding on in deep shade where taller trees crowd out most competitors. Summer and fall show it best.",
  "black-ash": "A tree of wet ground, black ash grows in swamps, fens, wet lowland forests and along slow streams, mainly through central and northern Ontario. You can separate it from other ashes by the leaflets, which sit directly on the stalk with no small stems of their own, and by the soft, corky grey bark that rubs and flakes under a thumb. The buds are nearly black.\n\nThe wood splits cleanly along its growth rings into thin, flexible strips, which is why it has long been central to Indigenous basket-making. It is now listed as Endangered in Ontario and protected under provincial law, so it should not be cut or damaged. The cause of the decline is the emerald ash borer, an invasive beetle that has killed ash across the province and left black ash populations falling steeply. Fall is the main season to notice it, though standing dead trees increasingly mark where it used to thrive.",
  "black-walnut": "Big compound leaves and round green nuts the size of a golf ball mark this southern Ontario native. It favours rich, moist bottomlands and valley soils, where it can reach 20 to 30 metres with a dark, deeply furrowed trunk. The wood is dark and richly grained, and it ranks among the most valuable hardwoods in North America, which has made large trees a target for theft in some areas.\n\nThe nuts are edible once you get past the thick husk, though the husk stains hands and clothes a lasting brown. That staining is harmless to touch. Less harmless to a garden is juglone, a compound the roots and leaves release into the soil. It suppresses tomatoes and many other garden plants growing within reach of the root zone, so what will grow under a walnut is limited. Late summer into fall, when the nuts drop, is the easiest time to find it.",
  "bur-oak": "Few trees shrug off as wide a range of conditions. Bur oak grows on floodplains, in old savannas and along prairie edges, tolerating both drought and periodic flooding, across southern and central Ontario. The acorns are the giveaway: large, half-buried in a deep cap fringed with a ragged, mossy edge unlike any other oak here. The leaves are big and fiddle-shaped, pinched in near the middle, and the bark forms thick corky ridges.\n\nThat bark is the key to how the tree lives. It is thick enough to insulate the trunk from grass fires that kill thinner-barked competitors, which let bur oak hold open savanna country before fire suppression changed those landscapes. Old trees grow broad and heavy-limbed in the open, narrower where forest closes in around them. Fall, when the acorns ripen and drop, is the easiest time to be sure of it.",
  "butternut": "Close kin to black walnut, butternut carries similar large compound leaves but sets a different nut: elongated and egg-shaped, sheathed in a sticky, hairy husk rather than a round one. The bark is grey and ridged, paler than its relative. It grows in rich, moist woods, on slopes and along streambanks through southern and central Ontario, usually as a scattered tree rather than a stand.\n\nIt is listed as Endangered in Ontario and protected under provincial law, so it should not be cut or damaged. The cause is butternut canker, a fungal disease that girdles branches and trunks and has killed a large share of the population, with no easy remedy. Healthy-looking trees are worth reporting, since disease-resistant individuals matter to recovery work. Summer and fall, when the nuts form and ripen, are the seasons you are most likely to notice one still standing.",
  "hackberry": "The bark tells you first. Grey and covered in corky, warty ridges and bumps, it is unlike anything else in southern Ontario woods. Hackberry grows on floodplains, along shorelines and on dry limestone soils, reaching 12 to 20 metres. The leaves are pointed and noticeably lopsided at the base, one side meeting the stalk lower than the other.\n\nSmall berry-like fruits ripen dark in fall, each about the size of a pea, with a thin sweetish flesh over a hard stone. They cling to the twigs well into winter after the leaves are gone, which makes the tree a reliable food source for waxwings, robins and other birds through the cold months. It is not a common tree here and tends to turn up singly along rivers and lakeshores. Summer and fall are when it stands out, though the warty bark identifies it year-round.",
  "sassafras": "One tree, three leaf shapes. On a single sassafras you will find plain ovals, two-lobed mittens and three-lobed leaves, all aromatic when crushed. The twigs are green and smell spicy when scratched. This is a southern, Carolinian species at the northern edge of its range in southwestern Ontario, where it grows in dry sandy woods and old fields, often spreading into thickets from root suckers. Reaching 10 to 20 metres, it turns clear orange and red in fall.\n\nThe plant has a long history of use for tea and flavouring, but caution is warranted here. The root bark and its oil contain safrole, a compound suspected of causing cancer and now restricted in food, so do not brew or eat any part of it. The tree itself is harmless to touch and pleasant to smell. Late summer and fall, with the mixed leaf shapes and strong colour, are the best times to know it.",
  "shagbark-hickory": "Mature bark peels away in long, curling vertical strips that hang loose from the trunk, and nothing else in southern Ontario looks quite like it. That shaggy plating gives the tree its name and its clearest field mark. Shagbark hickory grows in dry to moist upland forests, reaching 20 to 30 metres, with compound leaves usually carrying five leaflets.\n\nThe nuts are sweet and edible, held in a thick husk that splits into four sections when ripe, though squirrels and other animals usually reach them first. The loose bark matters to wildlife as well: bats roost in the gaps behind the peeling strips through the warmer months. Fall is when the tree comes into its own, dropping nuts and turning a warm yellow, but the bark reads clearly at any time of year. Young trees are smooth-barked and harder to place until they mature.",
  "tulip-tree": "The leaves settle identification on their own: four lobes with a broad, squared-off notch at the tip, as if the end had been clipped flat. In late spring the tree opens large tulip-shaped flowers, greenish with a band of orange at the base, though they sit high in the crown and are easy to miss from the ground.\n\nThis is one of the tallest hardwoods in eastern North America, reaching 25 to 35 metres, and in Canada it grows only in the rich, moist Carolinian woods of southern Ontario. It grows fast and straight, with a tall clean trunk, and it belongs to the magnolia family rather than the poplars that its other name, yellow-poplar, suggests. Spring for the flowers and fall for the clear yellow leaves are the times it stands out most. Look for it in the deep soils of the far southwest.",
  "white-oak": "Rounded leaf lobes, with no bristle tips, place this in the white oak group, and the pale, light-grey bark flaking in loose scales confirms it. White oak grows in dry to moist upland woods across southern Ontario, reaching 20 to 30 metres and living a long time. It grows slowly, and old open-grown trees develop broad, spreading crowns.\n\nThe wood is dense and, unlike red oak, has closed cells that hold liquid, which is why it was prized for barrels, ships and other work that had to stay watertight. Acorns ripen in a single season and carry less bitter tannin than red oak acorns, making them a favoured food for deer, turkeys and jays. Fall is the season to look, when the leaves turn a dull red-brown and often hang on the tree into winter.",
  "monarch": "Few insects are as well known as the monarch, and few make a journey as improbable. The orange-and-black butterflies that drift through Ontario gardens in late summer are the last of several generations born that year, and they will not breed here. Instead they turn south and fly, some of them more than four thousand kilometres, to a handful of mountain forests in central Mexico, where the whole eastern population gathers on the same fir trees their great-grandparents left the previous spring.\n\nTheir caterpillars eat only milkweed, storing the plant's bitter compounds so that birds learn to leave the adults alone. That single dependence is also the monarch's weakness. As milkweed has vanished from farm fields and roadsides, monarch numbers have fallen, and the species is now assessed as at risk. Planting native milkweed, and leaving it standing, is the simplest thing anyone can do to help."
};
